const DATA={"days": [{"stay": "Hotel Mayfair, Copenhagen", "car": false, "date": "Mon 28 Sep", "drive": "", "rise": "07:06", "set": "18:55", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "SQ248 05:55 + SQ352 00:05 → 07:10"}, "title": "Melbourne → Copenhagen", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "You land at 07:10", "t": "Off SQ352 into Kastrup at first light — after 24 hours in the air on SQ248 out of Melbourne yesterday morning (05:55) and the SIN → CPH red-eye. About a day out of Melbourne. Don't fight it — the whole job today is to stay upright until a local bedtime. The **M2 metro** runs straight from the airport to Kongens Nytorv in roughly 15 minutes, driverless and every few minutes, so you're in the centre before the city is properly awake.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Too early to check in, so leave the bags with the hotel and step straight back out. Keep it flat and slow: **Nyhavn**'s painted houses and the canal boats are ten minutes from Kongens Nytorv, and a harbour walk in daylight is the best thing you can do to reset three jetlagged bodies.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "**Sonny** on Rådhusstræde, or any Danish bakery, for a first proper coffee and a cardamom bun — the morning here is built for exactly this. Keep the caffeine early; you want to sleep tonight.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Hallernes Smørrebrød, Torvehallerne", "t": "Old-school stall inside Torvehallerne market — a proper fish smørrebrød (pickled herring, prawns, plaice) runs 60–75 DKK (~AU$13–16), eaten at a stool. The cheapest sit-down real Danish food in the city. 10 min walk from Hotel Mayfair.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · keep it gentle", "t": "A low-ambition first afternoon is the right call — one walk, one green space, no museum. Save the ambition for tomorrow, when everyone has slept.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Nyhavn back-lanes", "t": "If the toddler has any legs left after the harbour walk, slip off Nyhavn into **Kompagnistræde** and **Snaregade** — the 17th-century merchant lanes one block back from the water. Half of Copenhagen walks past the entrance without noticing. Ten quiet minutes of cobbles and pastel façades with almost no one on them.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Kongens Have", "t": "The King's Garden behind Rosenborg: lawns, gravel paths for small legs, a free **toy-soldier marionette theatre** on autumn afternoons, and the castle's moat and guards at the edge. Central and free — a soft landing rather than a headline.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:55", "t": "Eat early and by the water if the light holds — Nyhavn, or the harbour at **Ofelia Plads**. Then don't be heroes: get her down near a normal local bedtime, and get down yourselves. Tomorrow is the real Copenhagen day.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Jetlag, with a three-year-old", "t": "Daylight is the reset button. Get outside as much as you can today, push the first sleep to about 19:00 local, and accept a rough first night — the second is always better. Melbourne is 8–9 hours ahead, so her clock lands roughly right by evening.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Don't over-plan today", "t": "You have two full days here and this is not one of them — it's the half-day the flight left you. One walk, one park, one early dinner. Everything else is tomorrow.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Kastrup opened 1925 · Copenhagen airport is Europe's oldest", "Nyhavn houses since 1673 · Hans Christian Andersen lived at #67"], "shop": ["LEGO Duplo at arrivals · Sonny cardamom bun", "Illums Bolighus — note for tomorrow"], "story": ["The Little Mermaid — Andersen 1837 · why she has no legs", "Torvet at midnight · why the trolls left Copenhagen"]}}, {"stay": "Hotel Mayfair, Copenhagen", "car": false, "date": "Tue 29 Sep", "drive": "", "rise": "07:08", "set": "18:52", "title": "Copenhagen", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:08", "t": "If the jetlag has anyone awake, **Nyhavn at first light** is empty and gold — the row of gabled houses reflected in flat water, no crowd, no boats moving yet. Fifteen minutes, then back for breakfast.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · Bakken & Dyrehaven", "t": "Everyone has slept, so this is the day with ambition. Take the **S-train line C** from Nørreport to **Klampenborg** — 22 minutes, direct — and walk five minutes into the woods to **Bakken**, the oldest amusement park in the world (opened 1583). Free entry, pay per ride, and the whole park is sized for a three-year-old rather than a teenager: pony carousels, tea-cups, small ferris wheel, and the 1932 wooden **Rutschebanen** roller-coaster if she is brave. Two hours of rides, then walk out the north gate straight into **Dyrehaven** — the royal deer park, 2,000 wild fallow deer roaming the same forest since 1670, no fences, no cages, they walk to you. This is your Copenhagen-Zoo replacement, and it is the better trade — you skipped the polar bears for Polar Park anyway.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Bakken & the deer park", "t": "The oldest amusement park on Earth, and every ride is her scale — none of the queue-and-scream stuff. Then out the gate into **Dyrehaven**, where the fallow deer walk right up to strollers (bring apples, they know the sound of a plastic bag). October is rutting season, so you'll hear the stags calling across the beech woods. Free, and the memory that beats any zoo enclosure.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Lunch at the zoo, or back in town at **Torvehallerne**, the glass market halls by Nørreport — smørrebrød, coffee, and stalls to be walked past slowly. Central, covered, open all day.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Grod (Pølserytterens), Nørreport", "t": "Danish porridge bar — a warming bowl of øllebrød, oat or rice porridge with fruit and skyr for 55–75 DKK. Perfect breakfast/lunch that's not a smoked-fish gamble. Two branches within walking distance of the hotel.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Back in the centre, pick one thing and no more: **Rosenborg** and the crown jewels, the spiral climb up the **Rundetårn**, or the canal-boat loop from Nyhavn. One, unhurried.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Dragør", "t": "Twenty minutes south of Kastrup on Amager — a fishing village of yellow cottages and cobbled lanes preserved since the 1600s. Bus 250S from the city or a hire car for an hour after the zoo. Walk to the harbour, eat a smoked fish at Dragør Røgeri, and be back for dinner. Almost no international tourists.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Rain-day plan · Frederiksborg or Rundetårn", "t": "If Bakken is shut for the season (typically mid-October) or the sky is throwing water: **Frederiksborg Castle** at Hillerød is the swap. 40 minutes on S-train line A/E from Nørreport, a Renaissance castle floating on three linked lakes, ferry across to the island, chapel, royal apartments and a walled baroque garden. Open year-round, indoor-heavy, pram-accessible. In the city itself, **Rundetårn** is the quick indoor win — a 17th-century tower she spirals up on a smooth ramp rather than steps, ending on a rooftop over the old town.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:52", "t": "Last Copenhagen evening before the Arctic. Early dinner, pack tonight, and set an alarm — the Lofoten flight is a morning one and you don't want to be hunting for anything at 06:30.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Tomorrow is an early start", "t": "**SK2494 leaves Copenhagen 08:55** for Harstad-Narvik (Evenes), so be at Kastrup by about 07:15. Bags packed tonight, metro checked, hotel breakfast to-go if they'll do it. The trip proper starts tomorrow.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "No car in Copenhagen — you don't need one and parking is a tax. A hotel near Kongens Nytorv or Nørreport puts you on the metro and the S-train; the car is a Lofoten problem, collected at Evenes.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Bakken since 1583 — world's oldest amusement park", "Dyrehaven royal deer park since 1670 · 2,000 fallow deer wild"], "shop": ["Stilleben + Playtype for design", "Torvehallerne for fish smørrebrød"], "story": ["The Rutschebanen wooden coaster 1932", "Why the stags roar in October — rutting season"]}}, {"stay": "Airbnb Elisabeth-bua, Sørvågen", "car": true, "date": "Wed 30 Sep", "drive": "4h45", "rise": "07:26", "set": "18:50", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "SK2494 · 08:55 → 11:10"}, "title": "Denmark → Reine", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "No dawn · in the air", "t": "You land at 11:00. First light is spent over the Norwegian Sea. Tonight is a scout at Hamnøy, not a shoot — the real dawns start tomorrow.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "The day", "t": "Copenhagen 08:45, Evenes 11:00, car by 11:30, then the E10 west across the Lofast bridges through Svolvær and Leknes to Reine — 287 km and about 4h45 with stops. Late afternoon arrival with evening light still on the water.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "On the way", "t": "Austnesfjorden and the Sildpollnes church on its spit, about 90 minutes in. A proper 45-minute break at Svolvær harbour at the halfway mark.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Nusfjord on the way in", "t": "If you land Evenes on time and the light holds, turn south off the E10 at Napp for **Nusfjord** — a rorbuer village in a natural amphitheatre of rock, more preserved than Reine because it is a working port. Fifteen minutes off the E10, forty minutes on foot to see all of it, and you sleep an hour further along tonight.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Stop at the Coop or Rema 1000 in Leknes — an hour short of Reine and the last full-size supermarket. Rorbu cabins have proper kitchens and self-catering is the norm here, not a compromise. Stock up on salmon, prawns and lamb; October is peak Norwegian lamb.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Nothing much is open on the road west after Svolvær, so take the halfway break at Svolvær harbour — cafés around the marina, and the last reliable coffee before Reine.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Anita's Sjømat, Sakrisøy", "t": "Yellow cottage on the bridge to Reine — famous cod-burger 175 NOK, fish soup 200 NOK, or a smoked-salmon baguette 165 NOK (~AU$23). Best-value real Lofoten food on this side of the island. 10 min from your Airbnb.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "A travel day", "t": "Thirty-odd hours from Melbourne, so today the only job is to let her move — the Svolvær harbour break on the drive out, then the rorbu itself at Hamnøy, where the water, the boats and the bridge are world enough after a travel day.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:50", "t": "Hamnøy bridge, five minutes away. Scout it rather than shooting it. First aurora watch — read yr.no hour by hour; cloud decides everything, not the Kp number.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "Rorbu, not hotel. Book a cabin with a kitchen — self-catering is normal in Lofoten, not a compromise, and it saves you hunting for dinner after a 4h15 drive. Ask for parking at the cabin; it is almost always free and right outside.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "With her", "t": "After roughly 30 hours of travel from Melbourne, the 45-minute Svolvær stop is not optional. Get her out of the seat and moving.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Lofotfisket cod trade fed medieval Norway for 800 years", "The E10 fully joined the mainland only in 2007 (Lofast tunnel)"], "shop": ["Coop Extra Leknes — last cheap supermarket for 90 km", "Svolvær harbour — quick café stop on the drive"], "story": ["The stockfish trade · why every village has a drying rack", "Trolls in the Tjeldsund tunnels · a story for the drive"]}}, {"stay": "Airbnb Elisabeth-bua, Sørvågen", "car": true, "date": "Thu 1 Oct", "drive": "under 1h", "rise": "07:29", "set": "18:46", "transport": null, "title": "Reine", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:29", "t": "Hamnøy bridge. The most photographed view in northern Norway, ten minutes from your bed, empty at that hour. First of three attempts.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Back for breakfast, then Reine, Hamnøy and Sakrisøy on foot — the village, the rorbuer, the drying racks. Almost no driving, which is the point on day two.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Fifteen minutes to Sund, a working fishing village with a blacksmith's forge that most people pass. Then Å i Lofoten where the road simply ends — red buildings, a museum, a bakery.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Sakrisøy", "t": "The yellow-cottage islet on the bridge between Hamnøy and Reine — three ochre houses on a skerry, drying racks behind, Olstind above. **Anita's Sjømat** on the water sells the best smoked salmon in Lofoten and a bowl of fish soup for kroner nobody minds. Twenty minutes, no queues in October.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Three stops, all within twenty minutes of Reine.", "p": [{"name": "Sund Smie", "rate": "must", "desc": "A working forge where a blacksmith has hammered cormorant sculptures for decades. You watch the work and buy from the anvil. 15 min from Reine."}, {"name": "Bakeriet i Å", "rate": "good", "desc": "The oldest working bakery in Lofoten — cinnamon buns from a restored 19th-century wood-fired oven, at the literal end of the road."}, {"name": "Anita's Sjømat, Sakrisøy", "rate": "if", "desc": "Fish, king crab and stockfish from a yellow shed on the water. October hours shrink — call before driving over."}]}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Two, both at the far end of the road.", "p": [{"name": "Bakeriet i Å", "rate": "must", "desc": "Cinnamon buns from a restored 19th-century wood-fired oven — the oldest working bakery in Lofoten."}, {"name": "Café at Nusfjord", "rate": "if", "desc": "In the preserved village beside its 1907 general store. Modest, but the setting is a museum you sit inside."}]}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Reine Bakeri & Cafe", "t": "The old red boathouse cafe at Reine harbour — cinnamon buns 45 NOK, open sandwiches 90–130 NOK, fisherman's soup 195 NOK. Opens 07:00 daily. Cheapest full meal in Reine and the correct place for a mid-morning coffee.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Skagsanden beach", "t": "White Arctic sand at Flakstad, about 30 minutes north of Reine — flat, wide and safe for small legs, with mountains standing straight out of the sand. A beach at 68°N is not something she can do at home.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:46", "t": "Sakrisøy, five minutes — yellow rorbuer under Olstinden. Then Skagsanden, half an hour, for aurora.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Fuel", "t": "Fill up at Leknes or Ramberg. Stations are sparse west of Leknes and can be far apart on the run down to Å. Norwegian stations are card-only and unstaffed at night.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Rorbu cabins · winter fishing quarters since the 1400s", "Hamnøy is claimed as Lofoten's oldest village (16th century)"], "shop": ["Anita's Sjømat + Reine Bakeri", "Kaja Solheim prints at the Reine Tourist Info"], "story": ["The Draug — the drowned fisherman's ghost", "The sea eagles of Lofoten · wingspan wider than a person"]}}, {"stay": "Airbnb Elisabeth-bua, Sørvågen", "car": true, "date": "Fri 2 Oct", "drive": "1h30", "rise": "07:32", "set": "18:42", "transport": null, "title": "Reine — the far west", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:32", "t": "Reinebringen if the sky is clear — 1,566 sherpa steps, an hour up, leave 05:45. Not a hike to do with her. If it is grey, take Hamnøy again.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Offersøykammen. A 3.7 km return trek on Vestvågøy, moderate, about 1h30 with stops — a clear path up a low ridge with the reward at the top: Nappstraumen below you and the mountains of both islands laid out either side. Far more manageable than Kvalvika and a better view for the effort. Roughly 45 minutes from Reine.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "On the way", "t": "The Fredvang bridges — two white concrete arcs leaping between islands over open water, and you pass them on the way north. Pull in on the Ramberg side and shoot back along them. Everyone photographs Hamnøy; almost nobody stops here.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Nusfjord, the quietest of the preserved fishing villages, and Nesland, a near-abandoned hamlet reached on foot — a few buildings, no facilities, nobody there.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Å i Lofoten", "t": "Literally the last village — the E10 ends here. **Å** is a museum-village of tar-blackened cabins around a stockfish rack and a working bakery in a 19th-century boathouse. Buy the cinnamon bun. Thirty minutes past Reine and back, walkable in an hour end to end, the roundhouse view is the shot.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Nusfjord keeps a preserved 1907 general store and a working bakery inside the old village — one of the few places in Lofoten where the shop is the heritage site.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Nusfjord keeps a café in the preserved village alongside its 1907 general store. Modest, but the setting is a museum you can sit inside.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Bakeriet i Å", "t": "The working bakery inside the 1844 boathouse at Å — cinnamon buns 45 NOK, cardamom buns 50 NOK, cheese-and-brown-bread sandwich 65 NOK. Wood-fired ovens still going. Bring cash for the honesty bowl if unmanned in low season.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "The village of Å", "t": "The fishing village at the very end of the road — drying racks, red rorbuer and the old bakery still firing its 1844 wood oven for cinnamon buns. Small, flat and walkable: a working village she can wander, not a museum.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:42", "t": "Hamnøy or Sakrisøy, then aurora at Skagsanden or Flakstad. Third and last Reine night.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "On foot", "t": "Offersøykammen is a marked path, not a scramble, but the last stretch is steep and exposed to wind. A framed carrier is fine; a pushchair is not. Parking is a small lay-by on the Rv815 — arrive early, it holds about eight cars.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Å — the village literally called \"River\"; end of the E10", "Nusfjord preserved as a working port since 1907"], "shop": ["Bakeriet i Å in the 1844 boathouse", "Fisheries Museum stockfish woodblock print"], "story": ["How the trolls made the Lofoten wall in one night", "The last village at road's end — a story about stopping"]}}, {"stay": "Flex — Bardu / Narvik / Abisko, book on the day", "car": true, "date": "Sat 3 Oct", "drive": "5h east", "rise": "07:32", "set": "18:32", "transport": null, "title": "Reine → Polar Park (aurora chase east)", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:32", "t": "Hamnøy one final time. Best frame you have, and you leave today. Then straight onto the E10 east — this is a driving day, not a shooting day.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · out of Lofoten", "t": "Away from Sørvågen by 08:30. E10 all the way east — the same road you came in on, but now the light is behind you. Quick stops only: Reine viewpoint, Fredvang bridges, the Ramberg beach if the sky is doing anything. Push through Svolvær without stopping — you have skipped that hotel for a reason. Cross the Tjeldsund Bridge onto the mainland around 13:00 and turn south on the E6.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · Polar Park, Bardu", "t": "Northern Norway's wildlife park, and the only one where the enclosures are the size of small valleys. Wolves, brown bears, lynx, wolverines, elk and musk ox — the apex-predator lineup she will not see in Australia in a lifetime. Predator feeding walk in the afternoon (pre-book on polarpark.no); the standard visit is 90 minutes unaccompanied. In October the park closes at 16:00 — plan to arrive by 13:30 to have a full afternoon.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Ballstad or Nusfjord on the way out", "t": "You are driving the E10 east anyway — worth a fifteen-minute swing into **Ballstad** (a working fishing harbour the tour buses never make it to) or **Nusfjord** if you skipped it on the way in. Either sits ten minutes off the road. Ballstad has fewer photographers and more real boats.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "If aurora looks bad north — push east", "t": "From Bardu it is another ~2h east on E10 across the Swedish border to Abisko. The Torneträsk rain-shadow gives it the driest, clearest sky in Scandinavia and the best statistical aurora odds of anywhere on the route. The Aurora Sky Station chairlift runs evenings. Overnight in Abisko village if you go.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Nothing worth stopping for on this leg — save the browsing for when you come back through in Oslo.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Elverum Kafé i Bardu or the Polar Park café on site — both simple. If you push into Sweden, STF Abisko Turiststation serves reindeer stew in the mountain-lodge canteen until 20:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Polar Park Kafe, Bardu", "t": "Cafeteria at the park itself — moose stew 195 NOK, salmon soup 180 NOK, cheese-and-brown-bread rolls 65 NOK. Nothing gourmet, but a hot lunch after a 5-hour drive for less than a Svolvær dinner would cost. Kids under 6 free entry to the park.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Polar Park", "t": "Wolves and bears at eye level, in enclosures that look like the mountainside they came off. **The only place on this trip she will see a wolverine or a lynx.** Take the pram — the paths are gravel but flat.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:32", "t": "Aurora on the weather. Wherever you slept — Bardu, Setermoen, Narvik or Abisko — pick a north-facing lake or clearing away from streetlights. If Lofoten is clear behind you and Abisko clear ahead, sleep where the KP index says.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Book the room around 14:00", "t": "Do not lock in accommodation before you know what the sky is doing. Scandic Narvik, Thon Bardufoss and STF Abisko all take same-day bookings and none sell out on a random Saturday in early October. Booking.com free-cancellation rooms cover the pivot.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Border crossing", "t": "E10 into Sweden at Bjørnfjell is unmanned and passport-free (Schengen), but the Norwegian side has a customs stop for goods — nothing to declare if you are only carrying clothes. No vignette in Sweden. Roaming works across.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Polar Park opened 1994 · northernmost animal park at 68°N", "Bardu is officially bilingual · Norwegian and Northern Sámi"], "shop": ["Polar Park predator sheet — 85 NOK", "Coop Prix Setermoen · Vinmonopolet for Linie aquavit"], "story": ["The wolf and the moon · a Sámi bedtime tale", "How the wolves came back to Norway from 5 to 60"]}}, {"stay": "Flex — Bardu / Narvik / Abisko, book on the day", "car": true, "date": "Sun 4 Oct", "drive": "1–3h back toward Evenes", "rise": "07:34", "set": "18:28", "transport": null, "title": "Aurora day — Polar Park or Abisko", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Sleep in", "t": "The point of this day is tonight. Do not burn her on a dawn shoot.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "If Polar Park did not fit yesterday, do it this morning — gates open 10:00 in October. If you already did it, drive over to Abisko instead: the Aurora Sky Station chairlift, the STF trailhead onto Kungsleden, the view over Torneträsk. Either way, the middle of the day is a slow one.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · position west", "t": "Get yourselves back within 90 minutes of Evenes before dark. From Abisko that is ~3h30 via Narvik on E10; from Bardu ~1h30 south on E6. Do not leave this drive for tomorrow morning — the 15:40 flight to Oslo does not forgive weather delays on that road.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Rombaksbotn or Riksgränsen", "t": "If you are Narvik-side: drive out along **Rombaksbotn** on E10 west — the fjord finger where the German WWII wrecks lie, empty road, empty views. If you are pushing to Abisko: stop at **Riksgränsen**, literally on the Norway-Sweden border — the ski station is closed but the lookout above it gives you Torneträsk on one side and Norway on the other in a single frame.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Narvik if you are hedging", "t": "Narvik is the pragmatic middle — 1h from Evenes, a real town with food and hotels, and the Narvikfjellet cable car for an above-town aurora if the sky clears. Fair aurora odds and a short drive to the airport in the morning.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Nothing on this leg. Save shopping for the Oslo evening.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Rallarrosa or Rallaren in Narvik for a proper sit-down dinner before the aurora night. If you are in Abisko, the Turiststation dining room is the only real option and it is fine.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · STF Abisko Turiststation dining", "t": "If you push east: mountain-lodge canteen with a soup + salad + bread buffet at 129 SEK (~AU$19), open lunch and dinner. Reindeer stew and lingonberry on the plated menu 245 SEK. The only real food option in Abisko village — and it's good.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "A day off", "t": "One low-demand day between four hard travel days. Let her set the pace — playgrounds, the hotel pool if there is one, food she recognises.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:28 — aurora night", "t": "Whichever of Bardu, Narvik or Abisko has the clearest sky at 21:00 — the KP forecast at auroraforecast.is and the cloud map at yr.no both update hourly. This is the last aurora shot of the trip.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Tomorrow is the flight", "t": "Rental car has to be back at Evenes Hertz by 14:00 for a 15:40 departure. Fuel to full before you leave whichever town you slept in — the last stations near Evenes close on Sundays.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "If the sky is a write-off", "t": "Cut your losses and drive to Evenes for a bed near the airport (Radisson Blu Evenes is right there). One good night's sleep before the Oslo flight is worth more than a fifth cloudy aurora hunt.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Abisko rain shadow · 250 clear-sky nights a year", "Kristian Birkeland proved the aurora is charged particles, 1908"], "shop": ["STF Abisko + Aurora Sky Station shop", "Chad Blakley photos · Sámi duodji handicraft"], "story": ["Guovssahasat — the lights you can hear (Sámi taboo)", "Finnish revontulet — the fox-fires bedtime story"]}}, {"stay": "Comfort Hotel Børsparken, Oslo", "car": false, "date": "Mon 5 Oct", "drive": "3h", "rise": "07:37", "set": "18:38", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "DY365 · 15:40 → 17:25"}, "title": "Svolvær → Oslo", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:37", "t": "Svolvær harbour and the drying racks, walkable from anywhere in town. Low sun on the rorbuer and the fish flakes, and nobody about. Last Lofoten light of the trip.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Unhurried. Svolvær harbour in daylight, coffee, pack. Leave by 12:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "The drive east — 165 km, about 3h with stops. Sildpollnes church again on the way out. Car back by 16:30, evening flight.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Grünerløkka in Oslo", "t": "You land Oslo at 17:25 with the evening still open. Take tram 11 or 12 to **Grünerløkka** — the neighbourhood across the Akerselva from the centre, block-print bars and Sunday-in-Berlin cafés, streetart on every corner, **Mathallen** food market for dinner. Twenty minutes back to the Børsparken by tram or a €12 taxi.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "If you land with an hour to spare in Oslo: Mathallen Oslo at Vulkan, a converted iron foundry with three dozen food stalls, open until 20:00 on weekdays and a short tram from the centre.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Fuglen in Oslo is a 1963 interior serving coffee by day and cocktails at night, and it exports the model to Tokyo. Twelve minutes from Oslo S.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Vippa food hall, Akershusstranda", "t": "Waterfront hall of 8 stalls in a former ferry terminal — Kubus falafel 130 NOK, Vietnamese banh mi 120 NOK, fish taco 145 NOK. Under a bridge, view of Akershus, cheapest real dinner in Oslo. 15 min walk from Børsparken along the harbour.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Oslo Opera roof", "t": "You land at 17:25 and sleep on Oslo S. Ten minutes away, the **Opera House** lets you walk straight up its sloping marble roof — free, open late, the fjord all around. Exactly the run-around she needs after a flight.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Watch out", "t": "Stay on Oslo S, not at the airport. Tomorrow's train leaves Oslo S at 12:03, so you get a slow morning — but you still want to be walking downstairs, not riding the Flytoget in.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Rental return", "t": "Comfort Hotel Børsparken, Tollbugata 4 — 8 min walk from Oslo S, breakfast included. No car tonight (dropped at Evenes), so being right in the centre is the win.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "Comfort Hotel Børsparken sits on Tollbugata, one square inland from Karl Johans gate — 8 min walk from Oslo S. Breakfast is included. Do not lug bags to the platform in the morning; check out and stroll.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Battles of Narvik 1940 · 10 German destroyers sunk in 2 days", "Oslo has had three names: Ánslo, Kristiania, Oslo (1925)"], "shop": ["XXL Sport Oslo City · Norrøna House", "Freia chocolate · Grünerløkka independents"], "story": ["Askeladden — the Norwegian ash-lad who wins by paying attention", "Munch's Scream · painted after a walk on Ekebergveien 1892"]}}, {"stay": "Central Stay Apt 201-A, Bergen", "car": false, "date": "Tue 6 Oct", "drive": "no driving", "rise": "07:41", "set": "19:02", "transport": {"kind": "rail", "text": "Bergensbanen · 12:03 → 19:00"}, "title": "The Bergensbanen → Bergen", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "No dawn · slow morning", "t": "The train is not until 12:03, so this is the one morning to sleep in. Oslo's Akerselva path or the Opera House roof are ten minutes from the station if you wake early.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "The day", "t": "Oslo S 12:03, Bergen about 19:00. 496 km, 182 tunnels, over 300 bridges — out of Oslo through pine forest, up onto the Hardangervidda, the largest high-mountain plateau in Northern Europe, then down into fjord country. Highest point 1,237 m. Sit on the left; the right side is only better in the first and last hour.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Fjellveien terrace", "t": "You arrive Bergen around 19:00 — drop bags and walk fifteen minutes up **Fjellveien**, the terrace road above the centre. You reach the level of the funicular's halfway point for free, look down at the whole harbour lit for the night, and are back at the apartment inside the hour. Better than the queue for the Fløibanen tonight.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Villages from the window", "t": "Flå, old bear country · Gol and Ål in Hallingdal, dark-timber farms and stave-church country · Geilo · Finse at 1,222 m, the highest station in northern Europe with no road access at all · Myrdal, where the Flåm Railway branches off · then Voss.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Worth buying", "t": "Vy sells a GPS-triggered audio guide with the ticket. On a seven-hour ride with a three-year-old, it earns its price.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Seven hours on a train needs a plan: the Bergensbanen has a dining car, but the coffee is ordinary. Finse and Voss are the useful platform stops.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Bakeriet i Voss (station stop)", "t": "The Bergensbanen halts at Voss for 8 minutes — sprint across the platform to the station bakery for a fresh cinnamon bun (45 NOK) and coffee (35 NOK). Or, at arrival, Colonialen Kaffekompaniet in Bergen — sourdough sandwiches 95–125 NOK.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "The Bergensbanen", "t": "Seven hours across the mountain plateau with a dining car and room to move — far easier at three than a car, and the snowline and frozen tarns going past the window do the entertaining for you.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 19:02", "t": "You step off the train as the light goes. Blue hour at Bryggen runs to about 19:25 and the station is a ten-minute walk — drop the bags and go straight there if you want it. If the train runs late, no matter: you have tomorrow evening at Nordnes.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "Reserve seats when you book the train, and ask for the left side. Vy seat maps show orientation. Four westbound departures a day means a missed one costs you hours.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "With her", "t": "Seven hours is long. The dining car is a change of scene, and walking the length of the train twice is a legitimate activity at three.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Bergensbanen opened 1909 · crosses the Hardangervidda plateau", "Finse station at 1,222 m · highest point of any main line in northern Europe"], "shop": ["Voss station kiosk (8-min stop)", "Finse Amundsen expedition-photograph archive"], "story": ["The mountain trolls of Hardangervidda", "Why the train stops for reindeer on the tracks"]}}, {"stay": "Central Stay Apt 201-A, Bergen", "car": false, "date": "Wed 7 Oct", "drive": "no car", "rise": "08:19", "set": "18:59", "transport": null, "title": "Bergen", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 08:19", "t": "Bryggen's back alleys, walkable from a central hotel. Narrow and shaded, so soft early light suits them and there is nobody there at all.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "The fish market and Mathallen. Then one hill, not two: Fløibanen has a troll forest walk and goats at the top and is eight minutes up; Mount Ulriken is higher and the better frame. Pick one.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Nordnes and Skuteviken on foot — the steep wooden lanes above and beside Bryggen where Bergensers actually live. White clapboard, cobbles, laundry, almost no visitors. The same architecture as the postcard, with people in it. The best photography in the city and nobody does it.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Damsgård estate", "t": "Fifteen minutes across the harbour by bus 20 — **Damsgård Hovedgård**, a 1770 rococo wooden mansion painted white in a formal garden, one of Northern Europe's best-preserved wooden aristocratic houses. Almost no tourists find their way over. Wednesday afternoon guided tour in English at 14:00 if it runs; grounds free either way.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "All walkable from the apartment, in the order you would hit them.", "p": [{"name": "Mathallen Bergen", "rate": "must", "desc": "The indoor hall beside the harbour, open year-round and far better than the outdoor stalls once summer ends. Fish soup and a counter to sit at."}, {"name": "Global Food · Vestre Strømkaien 1", "rate": "must", "desc": "Rated the best Indian grocer in the city centre, directly across from Bergen Storsenter. Spices, dals, atta, frozen paratha."}, {"name": "Bryggen alley workshops", "rate": "good", "desc": "Leather, wool and ceramic makers tucked in the old wooden galleries. Julehuset is open all year."}, {"name": "Fjåk Chocolate", "rate": "if", "desc": "Chocolate shop and café. The correct answer to Bergen rain with a three-year-old."}]}, {"k": "also", "l": "If it pours — and it may", "t": "Bergen averages over 200 wet days a year. Troldhaugen, Grieg's villa with lunchtime recitals, is 20 minutes on the Bybanen. Fantoft stave church is on the same line. KODE 3 holds one of the best Munch collections outside Oslo.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Kaffemisjonen in the morning, Løvetann in the afternoon — they bracket the day.", "p": [{"name": "Kaffemisjonen", "rate": "must", "desc": "The city's specialty anchor, right by the Fløibanen entrance so it folds into your morning. Open 08:00–16:00 weekdays, 10:00–16:00 weekends."}, {"name": "Løvetann Kaffebar, Nordnes", "rate": "good", "desc": "A neighbourhood café among the wooden lanes, exactly where your afternoon walk goes. Licensed, serves food."}, {"name": "Det Lille Kaffekompaniet", "rate": "good", "desc": "Tiny place behind the funicular station, 11:00–18:00 daily — the answer when Kaffemisjonen has shut at four."}, {"name": "Colonialen Fetevare", "rate": "if", "desc": "Half café, half gourmet grocer. A slow lunch with food shopping attached."}]}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Pygmalion, Nedre Korskirkeallmenning", "t": "Bergen's longest-running vegetarian cafe — lentil dahl 155 NOK, halloumi burger 195 NOK, fish soup 170 NOK. Small, warm, locally loved. Around the corner from Bryggen. Cash and card, no service tip.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Fløibanen", "t": "Eight minutes up the funicular to a troll-forest walk and goats at the top. The ride itself is the treat; the easy loops and playground up there buy you an unhurried hour above the city.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:59", "t": "Nordnes point, where Bryggen's facade takes the last light across Vågen.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "Central hotel, no parking needed — you have no car until tomorrow morning. Anywhere between Bryggen and Nordnes puts everything on foot.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Money", "t": "Norway is effectively cashless. Cards work everywhere including small village cafés and the ferries; you will not need kroner in notes at all.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Bryggen UNESCO 1979 · Hanseatic trading post from 1360", "The wharves burned twelve times; rebuilt on the same 400-year-old timbers"], "shop": ["Bryggens Museum + Kunsthåndverk guild", "Fløibanen vintage-poster prints"], "story": ["The White Lady of Bryggen · a Hanseatic ghost", "Why it rains 240 days a year — a Norwegian excuse"]}}, {"stay": "Soltun Tinyhouse, Flåm", "car": true, "date": "Thu 8 Oct", "drive": "3h + stops", "rise": "08:22", "set": "18:56", "transport": null, "title": "Bergen → Aurlandsfjord", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 08:22", "t": "Bryggen once more before the car — the alleys are two minutes from the apartment and you leave at 08:30, so this is a fifteen-minute shoot, not an expedition.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Collect the car 08:30 from a city depot and take the E16 east through Voss. Tvindefossen is a roadside stop just beyond. Then Stalheimskleiva — thirteen hairpins dropping into Nærøydalen, one of the steepest roads in northern Europe.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "On the E16 · Dale of Norway outlet", "t": "If a snowflake sweater is going home, the **Dale of Norway factory outlet** in Dale (Vaksdal) is 55 min east of Flesland on the E16 you're driving anyway — no detour, straight on your line to Voss and Flåm. Cortina, Setesdal, Fana and Marius patterns at 30–50% off Bergen retail. Ring +47 56 59 41 00 the day before to confirm hours; the outlet has run reduced days since the brand sale in 2015. Thirty minutes covers it.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Gudvangen on the Nærøyfjord — UNESCO-listed like Geiranger, 250 m across at its narrowest with walls rising 1,400 m. Then Undredal: about 100 people, Norway's smallest stave church, and the goat cheese it has made for centuries. Then Flåm.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Vik i Sogn stave church", "t": "Twenty minutes off the E39 near Vangsnes — **Hopperstad Stave Church** (c.1130) is one of Norway's oldest and least visited. Dark tarred wood, dragon-head gables, painted interior. The ferry across to Vangsnes doubles as the fjord view of the day. Adds 45 min to the drive; almost nobody stops here.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Do not skip this", "t": "Otternes Bygdatun, on the shelf road between Flåm and Aurland — 27 restored farm buildings clustered on a terrace above the fjord, farmed from the 1600s and lived in until the 1990s. Grey timber, turf roofs, and the fjord dropping away below. It is directly on your road, it takes forty minutes, and it is the closest thing on this trip to what you liked about Undredal.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Undredal Stølsysteri — the farm dairy still making brown goat cheese by hand, with a small shop. This is the single most distinctive food purchase of your Norwegian leg. Ægir Bryggeri in Flåm is a stave-church-shaped brewery and taproom. Njord at Gudvangen for local crafts.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Ægir BrewPub, Flåm — built as a stave church, with carved dragons and an open fire. Coffee and cake in the afternoon, beer in the evening, and the warmest room on the fjord after Stegastein.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Furukroa cafeteria, Flåm", "t": "At the fjord ferry pier — self-serve cafeteria run by the fjord tour company. Salmon soup 179 NOK, fish cakes 165 NOK, waffles-and-brunost 65 NOK. Fjord view through the windows. Half the price of Ægir Bryggeri next door, and warmer.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Undredal goats", "t": "A village of a hundred people and rather more goats, making the brown cheese right there. Ten minutes off the fjord road — the kind of small, real, animal-filled stop that lands better at three than any castle.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:56", "t": "Stegastein — a timber ramp cantilevered 640 m above the Aurlandsfjord, twenty minutes up the Aurland valley. Go at golden hour; it faces west down the fjord.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "Ask for parking at Flåm or Aurland. Most hotels here have it free, but Flåm fills with cruise traffic even in October — confirm rather than assume.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Driving", "t": "Stalheimskleiva is one-way downhill only and closes with early snow. Check vegvesen.no. Stegastein is a narrow single-track climb with passing places — go slowly and yield uphill.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Aurlandsfjord UNESCO 2005 · a branch of the Sognefjord", "Undredal goat cheese made in the village since the 1400s"], "shop": ["Dale of Norway outlet on the E16", "Undredal Stølsysteri + Ægir Bryggeri Flåm"], "story": ["The goat of Undredal · why the cheese is brown", "Aurland stave church legends · elves in the timbers"]}}, {"stay": "Scandic Flesland Airport, Bergen", "car": true, "date": "Fri 9 Oct", "drive": "4h30", "rise": "08:12", "set": "18:57", "transport": null, "title": "Aurlandsfjord → Hardanger → the airport side", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 08:12", "t": "Stegastein again, or the Aurlandsfjord from the village. Twenty minutes up, and empty.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "The Aurlandsfjellet Snow Road over the plateau to Lærdal if it is open — a National Scenic Route across bare rock at 1,300 m. Otherwise back via Voss, which adds about forty minutes. Then south on Rv13 through Granvin.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Ulvik — a fruit village at the head of its own fjord arm, ringed by orchards, and mid-October is harvest and pressing week. Then west on the Rv7 to Steinsdalsfossen, where a made path takes you behind the falling water — no scramble, pushchair-friendly, and the best twenty minutes of the Norwegian leg for her.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Utne", "t": "On the Hardanger side, take the little **Utne** ferry across from Kvanndal — a fifteen-minute crossing to a village of white timber houses under Folgefonna, the **Hardanger Folk Museum** on the point (bunad costumes, rose-painting rooms) and the Utne Hotel from 1722 for coffee. Adds an hour; the ferry itself is the trip.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Steinsdalsfossen Kafe, Norheimsund", "t": "Right by the waterfall you can walk behind — heart-shaped waffles with brown cheese and jam 85 NOK, salmon soup 175 NOK, coffee 40 NOK. Country-cafe prices in the middle of nothing. Warms the drive to the airport.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Steinsdalsfossen", "t": "You walk **behind** the waterfall on a made, railed path — no scramble, just a tunnel of falling water. Twenty minutes, and the best single thing on the Norwegian leg for her.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:57", "t": "Carry on past Øystese and down to Scandic Flesland Airport, about 1h20, arriving around 20:00. Nothing here is scenic — that is the point. Tomorrow is the longest day of the trip and this removes 80 minutes from the drive to your morning flight.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Harvest and pressing week in Hardanger — this is the market day of the Norwegian leg.", "p": [{"name": "Syse Gård, Ulvik", "rate": "must", "desc": "Working fruit farm and cidery with a tasting room, farm shop and apple cake made from the trees outside."}, {"name": "Ulvik Frukt & Cideri", "rate": "good", "desc": "Family producer using Hardanger apples. Protected designation status, like Champagne."}, {"name": "Hardanger Saft- og Siderfabrikk", "rate": "good", "desc": "Presses juice as well as cider, which solves the three-year-old."}]}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Why here", "t": "Sleeping at the airport buys you an hour. From Øystese the 10th would start with an 80-minute drive before a flight, then Neuschwanstein, then three more hours to Austria. From Scandic Flesland it is a 15-minute walk to the terminal.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Watch out", "t": "The Snow Road is a summer road and closes with the first real snow. Check vegvesen.no that morning; the Voss fallback costs 40 minutes.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Hardanger fruit farms since the Cistercians brought grafts in 1147", "The Hardanger fiddle — 8 or 9 strings, national instrument since 1651"], "shop": ["Ulvik Frukt & Cideri · Hardanger Folkemuseum", "Steinsdalsfossen café — waffles with brunost"], "story": ["The maiden of Steinsdalsfossen", "Why Hardanger apples are red — a farmer's tale"]}}, {"stay": "Dorint Sporthotel, Garmisch-Partenkirchen", "car": true, "date": "Sat 10 Oct", "drive": "2h15 + 1h15", "rise": "07:31", "set": "18:38", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "WF1868 · 10:50 → 13:15"}, "title": "Bergen → Munich → Neuschwanstein", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "No dawn · airport morning", "t": "Fifteen minutes to Flesland and a 10:50 flight. Nothing scheduled by design — the shoot today is Neuschwanstein at golden hour.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Fifteen minutes to Flesland, drop the Tesla, flight 10:50. Munich 13:15, Mercedes at the terminal by 14:30. Route south on the A96 to Landsberg then down the B17 — the Romantic Road — so you can drop into the Wieskirche without doubling back. Into Hohenschwangau by 16:45.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "On the way · Wieskirche", "t": "Ten minutes off the B17 in a meadow outside Steingaden — a Rococo pilgrimage church so extraordinary that UNESCO listed a single building in a farmyard. Plain white walls outside, ceiling frescoes and gilt inside that stop you at the door. Open daily 08:00–20:00 in October, always free, no queues. Thirty minutes covers it. Add it to the drive to Neuschwanstein — you will not pass this way again.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · Neuschwanstein", "t": "Park at P4, beside Hohenschwangau and the Alpsee — about €12–15 for the day, and the only lot the shuttle serves. From there either take the shuttle bus up (€3.50 one way, €5 return, paid to the driver in cash, no cards) and walk the last 5 minutes to the Marienbrücke, or walk the whole way in about 45 minutes on a paved trail. The bridge is free, has no opening hours, and needs no castle ticket.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Oberammergau", "t": "If you leave Munich on the A95 south instead of the A7, drop off at Oberau for **Oberammergau** — the Bavarian passion-play village, whole houses painted floor-to-eaves with Lüftlmalerei fairy-tale scenes (Little Red Riding Hood, Hansel & Gretel on real walls). Twenty minutes off the route, half an hour to walk. Sits perfectly between the airport pickup and the Neuschwanstein arrival window.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Near Neuschwanstein · two minutes away each", "t": "Alpsee, the lake directly below Hohenschwangau — a flat shore path, turquoise water, and the castle above it from an angle nobody shoots. And Lechfall on the edge of Füssen, where the Lech drops through a narrow limestone gorge under a footbridge, five minutes from where you will eat. Both free, both empty at that hour.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "Coffee in Füssen", "t": "**Caffè Lucca** on Ritterstraße, five minutes from the Hohenschwangau car park in Füssen's old town — proper Italian espresso and a real breakfast, open to 18:00. Warm up here before the climb, or make it the reward between the castle and the drive east. It even opens Sunday 09:00–16:00, which almost nothing here does.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · EDEKA Füssen hot deli counter", "t": "Behind the Neuschwanstein car park, the town supermarket has a warm-food counter until 20:00: roast-chicken quarter €6.50, käsespätzle tray €4.90, breaded fish fillet €5.50, salad by weight. Eat at the picnic tables outside; the cheapest hot meal in Bavaria.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "One catch: **tomorrow is Sunday** and Germany and Austria both shut their shops. Today's only real window is the **EDEKA in Füssen**, minutes from the castle car park and open to 20:00 — hit it before the light turns, or you start the Salzkammergut with an empty kitchen and a dawn shoot ahead. Bread, coffee, breakfast, done.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Neuschwanstein & the Alpsee", "t": "The castle Disney copied — she is exactly the right age for that to land. Take the **horse-drawn carriage** up rather than the steep climb, and afterwards the flat Alpsee shore below is a turquoise lake she can walk right to the edge of.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:38", "t": "Shoot the castle from the bridge through golden hour. Then eat in Füssen and drive 1h15 east to Krün.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Arriving at Krün", "t": "You reach the Isar valley in the dark, but two things are worth knowing for the morning, both within ten minutes of where you sleep. Wallgau — rows of traditional houses painted with Lüftlmalerei, the pastel murals of Upper Bavaria, and from the bridge at the village edge the Isar winds through the valley with the peaks behind it. One writer who lives in the region calls it one of the best photo spots in this part of Bavaria. And Mittenwald, larger and famous for violin making since the 1680s, with painted facades along its main street.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "The shuttle stops before sunset", "t": "Summer timetable runs the last bus down about 18:45; the winter one stops at 16:45, and mid-October is exactly when it switches. Sunset is 18:38. Assume you may be walking down — 45 minutes, paved, but dark. Bring head torches. Check the day before at hohenschwangau.de/en/nature-surroundings/marys-bridge and neuschwanstein.de/englisch/tourist/howtoget.htm. The bridge also closes without notice in ice or high wind.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Vignettes", "t": "Buy the Austrian digital vignette online before you drive — you cross tomorrow. About €12 for 10 days, and a German-registered car will not have one. Hertz also charges €41.65 the moment you leave Germany.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Inside the castle", "t": "Summer hours run 09:00–18:00 to 15 October, so you are inside the window — but interior tours need booking about two days ahead and no photography is allowed inside. For one evening, the bridge is the better use of the time.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Ludwig II laid the Neuschwanstein foundation 1869 · died 1886", "Wieskirche UNESCO 1983 · Rococo pilgrimage church in a meadow"], "shop": ["Neuschwanstein museum shop", "Sport Conrad Garmisch · Bavarian nutcrackers"], "story": ["The Swan King · why he built the castle for himself", "The Starnberger See mystery · how he actually died"]}}, {"stay": "COOEE alpin Hotel Dachstein, Gosau", "car": true, "date": "Sun 11 Oct", "drive": "4h45 + stops", "rise": "07:35", "set": "18:36", "transport": null, "title": "Geroldsee → the Salzkammergut", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:35", "t": "Geroldsee. Twenty minutes from Garmisch, and the reason you slept here. You look south-east across the water at the Karwendel wall, with the wooden hay huts in the foreground — so the sun comes from behind you and lights the peaks directly while the lake holds them. Still water and alpenglow. This is the frame Gosausee was going to give you, and it is the better one.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · ten minutes each", "t": "Before you leave the valley: Wallgau for the Lüftlmalerei houses and the Isar bridge view, and Mittenwald, a violin-making town since the 1680s with painted facades down its main street. There is also a short panoramic path along the Isar from Krün itself with some of the best open views of the Zugspitze. Together about ninety minutes, and all of it on your way out.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "The gorge — the best one in Bavaria", "t": "Partnachklamm, fifteen minutes away in Garmisch. A 700 m slot cut 80 m deep, with the path pinned to the rock behind waterfalls and through tunnels, spray the whole way. It is the one gorge here open year-round — Höllentalklamm and the Leutascher Geisterklamm waterfall trail both close in mid-October. About an hour inside, plus a 20-minute walk in from the Olympic ski stadium car park. Bring the waterproofs; you will get wet.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Better than Partnachklamm, if you pick one", "t": "Eibsee. Fifteen minutes from Garmisch, turquoise water under the Zugspitze, and reasonably called the most beautiful lake in Germany. Go straight from Geroldsee. Parking is €2.50 if you leave before 09:30 and €10 after, and the approach road jams from about 10:00 — so this is a dawn-adjacent stop or not one at all. Geroldsee 07:35, Eibsee by 08:30, away by 09:30, and you are still in the Salzkammergut by mid-afternoon.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · the detour worth taking", "t": "The drive east runs past Berchtesgaden, and Ramsau bei Berchtesgaden is about 40 minutes off it. The Parish Church of St. Sebastian with the Watzmann peaks behind it is one of the most photographed churches in Germany, and the river trail from there leads to Hintersee and the Zauberwald — the Magic Forest. Best early morning or afternoon once the day-trippers have gone. \n\n**Wallfahrtskirche Maria Gern** — five minutes north of Berchtesgaden centre on the way in or out, so it slots into the same detour for essentially no extra time. A small white pilgrimage church with an onion dome on a hillside meadow, the Untersberg massif behind. Fifteen minutes on foot from the small car park below. Free, open daily, quiet on Sunday afternoon — Mass finishes by noon.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Then", "t": "On to the Salzkammergut. Drop bags at Obertraun or Bad Goisern, then into Hallstatt — the Salzberg funicular up to the Skywalk platform, and the Beinhaus beside the parish church, a charnel house of hand-painted skulls, named and dated. Ten minutes to see and unique in Europe.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Outlet worth the detour · Designer Outlet Salzburg", "t": "If the day is running to plan and one of you is buying jackets or shoes, the **McArthurGlen Designer Outlet Salzburg** sits at the Wals-Siezenheim exit of the A1, five minutes from Salzburg airport and right on your line east. One hundred-plus brands (Bogner, Fjällräven, Salomon, Peak Performance, Napapijri) at 30-60% off retail. Sunday closed everywhere in Austria — check hours the night before, since this is one of the few Sunday-trading exceptions and the rules move around. Thirty minutes covers it. Skip if the long driving day tomorrow needs the sleep.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Traunkirchen", "t": "Right on your route between Bad Ischl and Gmunden, a village on a small peninsula reaching into the Traunsee — **Traunkirchen**. The parish church holds a carved fisherman altar with the Apostles as fishermen pulling a net, and the lakeside path circles the point in twenty minutes. Ten minutes off the B145; nobody stops.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "**Konditorei Zauner** in Bad Ischl, on Pfarrgasse — the Kaiser's café since 1832, the largest cake buffet in Austria and the original Zaunerstollen. Open daily to 18:00, **Sundays included**, and Bad Ischl sits right on the road in. A proper 45-minute break here, half an hour short of Hallstatt, is the civilised way to break a 4h45 drive.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Bräuwirt Gosau or Bäckerei Maislinger, Hallstatt", "t": "In Gosau at the hotel end: Bräuwirt does käsespätzle €13, forelle müllerin €17, kaiserschmarrn €9. In Hallstatt for a lakeside snack: Bäckerei Maislinger — fresh bretzel €2, quark pastry €3.50, coffee €2.80. Cash preferred.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "You provisioned yesterday for a reason: **Sunday shuts almost everything here too**. The Eurospar in Bad Goisern is closed today and Hallstatt's tiny Nah & Frisch keeps short hours — the **SPAR by the Gosaumühle car park** is the one that tends to open for the tourist trade, and it's your only door if you're caught short. Otherwise, cook with what you carried in.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Hallstatt funicular & Eibsee", "t": "The Salzberg funicular and Skywalk are open to all ages, unlike the salt mine below it (barred under 4 by law). Earlier, the flat shore of **Eibsee** under the Zugspitze is a turquoise lake walk with nothing to climb.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:36", "t": "Hallstatt after five, when the coaches leave and it becomes a village of 700 again. Walk the lanes, eat by the water, and have the Marktplatz almost to yourselves. This is the hour that makes Hallstatt worth it.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "This day is now full", "t": "Geroldsee at dawn, then pick two of three: the Wallgau–Mittenwald loop, Eibsee, or Partnachklamm. Add the Berchtesgaden detour (Ramsau + Maria Gern, about 45 min off route together) and you are looking at just over 5 hours of driving. Leave the valley by 10:00 and you still reach Hallstatt for the empty hour after five.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Sleep outside the village", "t": "Gosau, about 30 minutes from Hallstatt via the western lake road. Hallstatt's centre is car-free, parking is about €15 a day at P1, and you called it too touristy — which it is, between nine and five. Staying outside gives you the good hours without the logistics.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Not an option", "t": "The salt mine bars children under 4 by law, without exception. The funicular, Skywalk and Rudolfstower above it are open to all ages and are the better photograph anyway.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Tomorrow rolls early", "t": "Krumlov gets a full day and dinner tomorrow, then a two-hour motorway leg to Prague after dark. That works because the three-year-old sleeps in the car seat — but only if today ends soft. Aim for bags packed tonight, lights out by 21:00, out of Gosau by 08:30. The Hallstatt dawn viewpoint slots straight into that plan; if you skip it, aim for a 09:00 departure.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Hallstatt salt mine · continuously worked for 7,000 years", "The Salzkammergut UNESCO 1997 · imperial Habsburg salt monopoly"], "shop": ["Salzwelten Hallstatt kids' kit", "Konditorei Zauner Bad Ischl — Kaiser's café since 1832"], "story": ["Kaiserschmarrn — the emperor's mess · why Franz Joseph loved it", "The Beinhaus at Hallstatt · painted skulls, named and dated"]}}, {"stay": "Wenceslas Square apt, Prague", "car": true, "date": "Mon 12 Oct", "drive": "6h + stops", "rise": "07:38", "set": "18:33", "transport": null, "title": "Hallstatt → Admont → Krumlov → Prague", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:38", "t": "The classic Hallstatt viewpoint, on the lakeside road at the north end of the village — so you do not need to enter the car-free zone at all. About 25 minutes from Gosau. Mist on the lake if you are lucky. Drone ban in force; photography discouraged in the residential lanes.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · via Bad Ischl to Admont", "t": "Away from Gosau by 08:15. East through Bad Ischl (coffee stop below), then the B145/B146 into the Ennstal to **Stift Admont** for the library opening at 10:00. About 2h drive. The Admont Abbey Library is the largest monastic library in the world — a 70-metre Baroque hall with seven ceiling frescoes and shelves under gilded stucco. Ninety minutes inside covers the library, the natural history cabinet and the contemporary art wing. On the road by 12:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · Admont → Krumlov", "t": "North out of Admont via Amstetten, west of Linz, and up to Freistadt just short of the Czech border — 3h drive with one break. Freistadt is the natural coffee-and-stretch stop; a walled medieval town where the fortifications are still intact. Into Český Krumlov by 16:00 — a UNESCO town wrapped inside a horseshoe bend of the Vltava, castle above, painted tower over everything. Compressed visit: viewpoint above the river bend, walk down through the old town, bears in the castle moat, dinner on the river.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Vyšší Brod", "t": "Fifteen minutes south of your route between the Austrian border and Krumlov — **Vyšší Brod**, a Cistercian abbey founded in 1259 on the Vltava. Small library, gothic cloister, and a Madonna painting the Nazis stole and ČSSR recovered. If you leave Admont on time you have half an hour spare for it before Krumlov; if not, skip.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Krumlov's bear moat", "t": "Český Krumlov castle has kept **brown bears** in its dry moat for centuries — you look down at them from the bridge into the castle, free and no queue. Three bears there now, and it is the small visitors they draw. A proper stretch-and-look on the drive to Prague.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · early dinner, then the drive", "t": "Sunset 18:33. Eat by 17:30 so you can be on the road by 19:00. Dinner on the river — **Papa's Living Restaurant** on Latrán has a terrace over the Vltava and a kids' menu; **Krumlovský Mlýn** below the castle is the classic Czech option. Book from Gosau before you leave — Monday evening still fills. Prague apartment by ~21:30.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "What Admont costs you", "t": "Adding Admont means dropping the Traunsee/Gmunden detour and the Schloss Ort footbridge that used to sit on this route, and compressing Krumlov from four hours to two. The library is worth all of that. If you decide against it, the old route via Bad Ischl → Gmunden → Freistadt gives you Krumlov by 14:30 and a full afternoon there.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Skip the Gmundner Keramik detour on the Admont route — you cannot afford the twenty minutes. Buy the ceramics at the Vienna airport shop on the way home if she wants them.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Konditorei Zauner, Bad Ischl — founded 1832, imperial court confectioner to Franz Joseph, still making Zaunerstollen in the same rooms. Forty minutes into the drive and the only fixed coffee stop of the day. In and out in twenty minutes.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Papa's Living Restaurant, Latrán Český Krumlov", "t": "The terrace over the Vltava you've already got for dinner — lunch/afternoon menu is cheaper: veg goulash 189 CZK, trout 289 CZK, kids' fish-and-chips 149 CZK. Coffee-and-cake 95 CZK. Book anyway; Monday still fills.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Admont — hours and tickets", "t": "Library and museums open daily 10:00–17:00 in October, last entry 16:30. Adult €14.50, under-6 free. No booking needed on a Monday morning — walk up. Photography allowed without flash. Pram-accessible via the lift.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Border", "t": "Buy the Czech vignette before you cross — edalnice.cz, or the last Austrian services. Plates are checked automatically and fines are immediate.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Parking in Krumlov", "t": "Krumlov's old town is largely pedestrian. Use the P2 lot below the castle and walk in — five minutes, and it saves navigating medieval lanes.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Book dinner before you leave Gosau", "t": "Krumlov's better riverside tables go early even on a Monday in October. Book Papa's or Krumlovský Mlýn from the Gosau breakfast table — it locks the day's shape and takes the decision off the toddler at 18:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Swap the driving", "t": "This is the longest driving day of the trip — six hours behind the wheel plus a library, a walled town and Krumlov. Split it in two between you: one drives Gosau → Admont, the other Admont → Krumlov, then swap again for the night leg to Prague.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Stift Admont library 1074 · largest monastic library in the world", "Český Krumlov UNESCO 1992 · castle above a Vltava horseshoe bend"], "shop": ["Admont library shop", "Krumlov marionette workshops · Krumlov Rose ceramics"], "story": ["The three brown bears of Krumlov Castle · kept since the 1500s", "Altomonte's 1776 frescoes · the ceiling that never ends"]}}, {"stay": "Wenceslas Square apt, Prague", "car": true, "date": "Tue 13 Oct", "drive": "no car", "rise": "07:39", "set": "18:27", "transport": null, "title": "Prague — the old city", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:39", "t": "Charles Bridge, empty. Fifteen minutes on foot from Wenceslas Square — by nine it is shoulder to shoulder, and at seven it is thirty statues and the river. Worth the walk; this is the one alarm in Prague to set.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Prague Castle and St. Vitus early, then down through the lanes. Or the Old Town Square and the Astronomical Clock — go before nine either way.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Petřín Hill — the funicular up, a mirror maze and a lookout tower at the top, gardens the whole way down. The best afternoon in Prague at her age. On the way, two things almost nobody finds: Nový Svět, a lane of small coloured cottages behind the Castle where Kepler and Tycho Brahe lived — two minutes off the tourist route and silent — and the Strahov Library, two Baroque halls of painted ceilings and globes that are among the most beautiful rooms in Europe. Kampa Island and the Čertovka mill race are five minutes from Malá Strana and feel like a village inside the city.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Nový Svět", "t": "One cobbled lane behind Prague Castle, downhill from Loreta — **Nový Svět** (New World), where Tycho Brahe and Kepler lived. Six houses along a curved lane, no shops, no crowds, and you are already up there for the castle. Ten minutes; the frame is the whole street empty at the end of the day.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Two markets and two shops, all central.", "p": [{"name": "Havelské tržiště", "rate": "good", "desc": "Trading since 1232 and the oldest open-air market in Prague. Produce at one end, crafts at the other. Daily."}, {"name": "Kubista · House of the Black Madonna", "rate": "must", "desc": "A shop inside the world's only Cubist building, selling reproductions of Cubist furniture and ceramics. Nowhere else does this."}, {"name": "Manufaktura", "rate": "good", "desc": "Czech wooden toys, marionettes and beeswax. Several branches; the Melantrichova one is nearest."}, {"name": "Strahov Library shop", "rate": "if", "desc": "Prints and facsimiles from two of the most beautiful Baroque rooms in Europe."}]}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Two for the rooms, two for the coffee.", "p": [{"name": "Café Savoy", "rate": "must", "desc": "1893, a neo-Renaissance ceiling seven metres up, the best breakfast in Prague, and explicitly relaxed about small children. Book ahead."}, {"name": "Café Louvre", "rate": "must", "desc": "1902, red interiors and antique tables. Kafka and Einstein drank here and it is still full of locals rather than tour groups."}, {"name": "Kavárna Slavia", "rate": "good", "desc": "The city's oldest in near-continuous use since 1884, on the riverbank facing the National Theatre. Go for the view across to the castle."}, {"name": "Onesip or EMA Espresso Bar", "rate": "good", "desc": "If you want the actual best coffee rather than the best room."}]}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Sisters Bistro, Dlouhá 39", "t": "Chlebíčky (open sandwiches) from 45 CZK each — salmon-and-egg, herring-and-remoulade, potato salad with roe. Two feed a light lunch for under 250 CZK (~AU$16) for two. Stand-up counter and a couple of stools. Best cheap Czech eating in Old Town.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Petřín Hill", "t": "Funicular up, a mirror maze, a lookout tower like a little Eiffel, and gardens to walk down through. The best afternoon in Prague at her age — and the funicular ride is half of it.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:27", "t": "Letná Park above the river bend — the whole city and its bridges at dusk, and a beer garden with the best view in Prague. Or Riegrovy sady in Vinohrady, a slope of grass looking west at the Castle where Praguers actually go after work — the same skyline, none of the visitors, and she can run about while you shoot.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "With her", "t": "The Petřín funicular takes standard Prague transit tickets, so a day pass covers it. The mirror maze at the top is small, cheap and exactly right at three.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Timing", "t": "Charles Bridge at 07:39 is empty; by 09:00 it is not. This is the one alarm in Prague worth setting.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Charles Bridge 1357 · Old Town Square from the 12th century", "Astronomical Clock 1410 · the oldest still-working mechanical clock in the world"], "shop": ["Kubista at the House of the Black Madonna", "Krtek at Palladium · Papelote on Vojtěšská"], "story": ["Rabbi Loew and the Golem of Prague · the Old-New Synagogue", "The Astronomical Clock apostles · why 09:00–23:00 hourly"]}}, {"stay": "Wenceslas Square apt, Prague", "car": true, "date": "Wed 14 Oct", "drive": "no car", "rise": "07:41", "set": "18:25", "transport": null, "title": "Prague — the day the third night bought", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:41", "t": "Vyšehrad — the old fortress on a bluff south of the centre, with a neo-Gothic basilica, the cemetery of Czech composers and writers, and ramparts looking down the Vltava. Almost no visitors at any hour, let alone this one. Twenty minutes by tram, or a walk along the embankment.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning — pick one", "t": "Kutná Hora, just under an hour by train from Praha hlavní nádraží, which is a few minutes from where you are staying. St. Barbara's is one of the great Gothic churches of Europe, and the Sedlec Ossuary is decorated with the bones of some 40,000 people — extraordinary, and your call whether it suits her. Half a day to a full one.\n\nOr Prague Zoo, consistently ranked among the best in the world, spread over a hillside in Troja with a chairlift, and reachable by boat up the Vltava — which is half the fun. This is the replacement for the Schönbrunn day you gave up when Vienna came out.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon", "t": "Whichever you did not do in the morning, or simply stop — this is day fifteen and nobody needs a schedule. Kampa Island and the Čertovka mill race feel like a village inside the city, and the Vrtba Garden is a Baroque terrace almost nobody finds.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Vyšehrad", "t": "The *other* Prague castle hill, three tram stops south along the river — **Vyšehrad** fortress. Ramparts you can walk, the SS Peter and Paul basilica lit up at dusk, and the view back across the Vltava to Prague Castle that most tourists never see. Free, always open, prams roll on the paths. An hour end to end.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Prague Zoo", "t": "Genuinely world-class, built into a hillside, with a **chairlift** over the enclosures and a boat up the Vltava to reach it. Even with Melbourne Zoo at home, this one earns the day.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:25", "t": "Letná Park above the river bend — the whole city and its bridges at dusk, and a beer garden with the best view in Prague. Repack tonight.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Wednesday to Saturday only, so today works.", "p": [{"name": "Jiřího z Poděbrad farmers market", "rate": "must", "desc": "Under Plečnik's extraordinary church in Vinohrady, two stops on the A line. Where Praguers shop rather than tourists. Wed–Sat."}, {"name": "Náplavka riverbank", "rate": "if", "desc": "If you are down by the water — food stalls and boat bars along the embankment."}]}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Café Louvre on Národní — 1902, red interiors and antique tables, where Kafka and Einstein drank, and still full of locals rather than tour groups. Kavárna Slavia on the riverbank for the view across to Charles Bridge and the castle. For actual coffee rather than the room: Onesip or EMA Espresso Bar.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Havánska Kachna vegetarian branches / Bageterie Boulevard", "t": "For a proper Czech vegetarian lunch: Have Some Salt on Sokolská — mains 195–245 CZK. For fast-and-cheap: Bageterie Boulevard (Wenceslas Sq branch), Czech chain baguettes 110–160 CZK, tuna-and-egg or halloumi options, open 08:00–22:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Tickets", "t": "Kutná Hora trains run roughly hourly from the main station. Book the Sedlec Ossuary slot online — it is small, timed, and sells out.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "With her", "t": "The Prague Zoo boat from Rašínovo nábřeží takes about 75 minutes each way and is worth it as the journey. Check it is still running in mid-October before committing the morning.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Vyšehrad · the other Prague castle, and the older one", "Prague Castle · officially the largest ancient castle in the world (Guinness)"], "shop": ["Fashion Arena Prague Outlet — dedicated trip", "Museum Kampa · Josef Sudek photograph prints"], "story": ["Libuše — the princess who founded Prague from a vision", "The Defenestrations — Prague's way of settling arguments"]}}, {"stay": "Amedia Plaza, Dresden", "car": true, "date": "Thu 15 Oct", "drive": "2h, mid-morning", "rise": "07:43", "set": "18:14", "transport": null, "title": "Prague morning → Dresden", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Optional dawn · 07:43", "t": "One last Prague dawn is optional — you did the sunrise on Charles Bridge and from Letná on your other two mornings. Sleep in this time. The day starts after breakfast.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · breakfast, then north", "t": "Breakfast at the apartment or **The Miners** on Lázeňská (below), pack, keys back in the safe. On the road by 09:30. Straight up the D8 across the border — two hours to Dresden, in by 11:30. You gain a full afternoon in the city this way.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · a proper day in Dresden", "t": "Check in at the Amedia — bags with reception if the room is not ready. Lunch at Grand Café Coselpalais beside the Frauenkirche, or Ladencafé Aha on Kreuzstraße if you want something lighter. Then the day belongs to Dresden: the Frauenkirche interior first (free, quieter after 13:00), then the **Zwinger** — inside this time, not just the courtyard. The **Gemäldegalerie Alte Meister** holds Raphael's Sistine Madonna (yes, the two putti), Vermeer's Girl Reading a Letter and the best German Renaissance rooms outside Berlin. Two hours for the gallery, another 30 min in the Porzellansammlung next door. Out by 16:30 for coffee, then the dusk shoot.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Litoměřice on the D8", "t": "Thirty minutes into your Prague-Dresden drive, exit the D8 at Lovosice for **Litoměřice** — a Renaissance walled town on the Elbe, sloping arcaded main square, no coach parties. Fifteen minutes to walk the square and back at the car. If you would rather remember it, **Terezín** memorial (five minutes on) is the other stop — heavy, not for the toddler but historically important.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Zwinger courtyard at blue hour", "t": "After the interior, come back through the Zwinger courtyard around 17:30 — the museum ticket booth is shut, the tourist mass has thinned, and the Kronentor colonnades catch the last usable light. Ten minutes, then out the west side to Theaterplatz and on to Brühl's Terrace as the sky goes.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "**The Miners** on Lázeňská, two minutes off the Malá Strana end of Charles Bridge — a Prague specialty roaster with a terrace that catches the morning, open from 08:00. Warm up here straight after the dawn shoot, over eggs and a real flat white, before you point the car north.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Ladencafé Aha, Kreuzstraße", "t": "Two minutes off Neumarkt — Saxon regional cooking in a converted grocery store, salmon fillet with quarkkeulchen €14.90, käsespätzle €11.50, forelle €16.90, kids' pancakes €6.90. Good wine list; unfussy, packed at lunch.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "For the road", "t": "You are lunching in Dresden, not out of the boot, so nothing to stock. Coffee-and-pastry to go from The Miners or the apartment coffee is enough for the two-hour drive. Fill the tank in Prague — diesel is cheaper on the Czech side of the border.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Dresden Parkeisenbahn", "t": "In the Großer Garten, a miniature railway **run by children** — a real 30-minute ride behind small engines, open afternoons to the end of October (Thu–Fri from 13:00, weekends from 10:30). Slot it before the evening shoot, or hold it for tomorrow.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:14 — the shoot", "t": "This is the Dresden evening. Start at the Fürstenzug on Augustusstraße — 102 metres of Meissen porcelain tiles, 25,000 of them, showing a thousand years of Saxon rulers on horseback. Narrow street, so shoot it along the wall rather than square on. Then Brühl's Terrace as the sky goes, and cross to the Neustadt bank for the classic frame: the Frauenkirche dome, the Hofkirche and the Ständehaus lit and reflected in the Elbe. Blue hour runs to about 18:50, and the floodlights come on before it ends — that overlap is the shot.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Three vantages, in order", "t": "Augustusbrücke — on the bridge itself, the whole skyline in one line. Neustädter Elbufer — the grass bank downstream of the bridge, lower angle and more reflection. Carolabrücke — further east, gives you the terrace and the dome stacked. Tripod for all three.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Why leave straight after breakfast", "t": "The 09:30 departure is what buys you the full Dresden day. Leave at 11:00 and you lose the Alte Meister interior before the light shifts; leave at 14:00 (the old plan) and Dresden shrinks to just the dusk shoot. Breakfast, pack, drive.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "The cost", "t": "You lose the Prague afternoon and Bohemian Switzerland on the drive. The Bastei is 40 minutes from Dresden, so pick it up tomorrow morning if you want it.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Parking", "t": "Confirm the Amedia's garage takes a late arrival. Most German city hotels do, but check the overnight rate — some charge more than the room is worth.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Frauenkirche flattened 1945, rebuilt stone by stone, reopened 2005", "Zwinger 1710s · Meissen porcelain invented 1710, same year"], "shop": ["Elbepark Dresden on the D8 arrival", "Meissen factory outlet · seconds at 30–50% off"], "story": ["The Sistine Madonna's two putti · the meme cherubs of Dresden", "Why the city was firebombed · February 1945"]}}, {"stay": "Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers", "car": false, "date": "Fri 16 Oct", "drive": "2h, late afternoon", "rise": "07:31", "set": "18:07", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "D83305 · 21:55 → 22:55"}, "title": "Dresden, all day → Berlin airport", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:31", "t": "The Augustusbrücke — the whole Baroque skyline from across the Elbe, empty, with mist on the river if you are lucky. Five minutes from the Altstadt and the best frame in the city.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning · Semperoper + Neustadt", "t": "You did the Zwinger properly yesterday, so this morning is what you didn't fit. Book the **Semperoper backstage tour** (45 min, English at 11:00, ~€14) — one of Europe's three greatest opera houses, and the stage machinery is the story. If tours are full, walk across to **Residenzschloss** for the Grünes Gewölbe (Historic and New Green Vault, separate tickets) — August the Strong's treasury and the greatest hoard of jewelled objects in Europe. Either fills the morning.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Outlet worth the detour · Meissen porcelain factory", "t": "Thirty minutes north-west of Dresden by S1 train or car — the **Porzellan-Manufaktur Meissen** itself, the actual kilns of the oldest European porcelain house (1710). The factory demonstration studios and the museum are one ticket (~€12), and the **outlet room upstairs** carries seconds and end-of-line at 30-50% off flagship prices — the famous crossed-swords mark for a fraction of what the Altstadt shop asks. Two hours end to end. If Meissen porcelain is going home in a suitcase, this is where to buy it.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Or, if you want the gorges", "t": "Bastei is 40 minutes upriver — a sandstone bridge strung between rock towers 190 m above the Elbe, and the thing you gave up by driving at night. It needs about an hour on foot plus the drive, so it replaces the Neustadt afternoon rather than adding to it.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · Neustadt", "t": "Across the river, and the antidote to the rebuilt Altstadt. Kunsthofpassage — linked courtyards of independent shops, including the wall of funnels and gutters that plays music when it rains. Pfunds Molkerei, an 1880 dairy tiled floor to ceiling by Villeroy & Boch and widely called the most beautiful shop in the world.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Radebeul", "t": "Fifteen minutes west of Dresden by S-Bahn or car — **Radebeul**, Saxony's winemaking suburb. Half-timbered houses, terraced vineyards climbing behind, and the **Karl May Museum** in a wooden lodge that looks like a set for the westerns he wrote (Old Shatterhand novels loved across Germany). The **Sächsische Weinstraße** ends here. Fits before you set off for Berlin if you can spare 90 minutes.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "The funnel courtyard", "t": "In the Neustadt, the Kunsthofpassage's Hof der Elemente — a house wall plumbed with funnels and gutters that **plays music when it rains**. Whimsical, free to wander, and already on the afternoon's Neustadt route.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Late afternoon · leave by 16:30", "t": "Coffee at Pfunds Molkerei's upstairs café, then back over the Elbe. Two hours to Berlin Brandenburg, arriving around 18:45. Return the car, check in, flight 21:55. Copenhagen near 23:00.", "p": []}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Grand Café Coselpalais, a Baroque palace beside the Frauenkirche with a cake trolley and a terrace facing the church. Then Pfunds has a small café upstairs — go for the room.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Lila Soße, Kunsthofpassage", "t": "Neustadt courtyard restaurant known for \"Saxon in a jar\" — small dishes in mason jars €4.90–12 each. Pick four to share for two people at ~€30 total. Trout-quark tartare, potato-mushroom soup, quarkkeulchen. Vegetarian-heavy, no beef/pork if you pick around it.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Zwinger — done yesterday, but", "t": "If yesterday's Alte Meister visit ran short, both galleries reopen 10:00 Friday (closed Mondays only). Combined ticket ~€14 adult, under-17 free. Otherwise walk through the courtyard once more on the way to lunch — it takes ten minutes and it is worth doing twice.", "p": []}, {"k": "warn", "l": "Do not cut this fine", "t": "Sunset is 18:07 and the flight is 21:55. Leave Dresden by 16:30, not at sunset — that puts you at BER around 18:45 with time to return the car properly and eat before the gate. Leaving at 18:00 would land you there at 20:10 with a car to hand back and a three-year-old to board, which is not the evening you want.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Rental return", "t": "Domestic German one-way, so the cheap kind — but photograph the car and get a signed receipt. You are flying out the same evening and cannot come back to argue about a scratch.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "What you give up", "t": "Berlin entirely, including the Türkischer Markt on Maybachufer, which runs Fridays. It was the best market of the trip — but it would have cost you the Dresden day, and Dresden is the better city to actually see.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Semperoper 1841 · one of Europe's three greatest opera houses", "The Grünes Gewölbe · Europe's greatest jewelled-object treasury"], "shop": ["Meissen factory outlet (last-chance morning trip)", "Frau Wirth Papeterie · Semperoper stage-set shop"], "story": ["August the Strong · Elector of Saxony who bought porcelain by the ton", "Kunsthofpassage · the wall that plays music in the rain"]}}, {"stay": "Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers", "car": false, "date": "Sat 17 Oct", "drive": "no car", "rise": "07:47", "set": "18:17", "transport": null, "title": "Copenhagen — rest day", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "Dawn · 07:47 — optional", "t": "You landed at 23:00. Sleep in. If you are awake anyway, Nyhavn before eight is empty and the light comes down the canal from the east.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "Morning", "t": "Sleep in. Then Torvehallerne, the glass market halls at Nørreport — sixty-odd stalls, open Saturday, and the easiest good lunch in the city.", "p": []}, {"k": "aft", "l": "Afternoon · Experimentarium", "t": "The big hands-on science museum out at **Hellerup** — three floors of things to push, pull, build and climb, a giant Bubble room, and a body-and-senses floor pitched right at her age. S-train from Nørreport to Hellerup, about twelve minutes, then a short walk to the water. Allow a good half-day; it's the last real outing of the trip.", "p": []}, {"k": "detour", "l": "Detour worth taking · Louisiana Museum", "t": "Thirty-five minutes north on the coast train (Kystbanen) from Copenhagen H to Humlebæk — **Louisiana Museum of Modern Art**. Danish modern architecture as good as anything inside; sculpture park sloping down to the Øresund with Sweden across the water. She will run in the grass while you look at Giacometti. Half-day out and back, worth every minute.", "p": []}, {"k": "also", "l": "Instead, if she'd rather animals", "t": "**Den Blå Planet** — Northern Europe's largest aquarium, one metro stop from the airport at Kastrup, timed entry, book online. Both eat an afternoon and you have one; take the Experimentarium for something Melbourne can't hand her, and let Den Blå Planet double as tomorrow's dry run to the airport if you'd rather.", "p": []}, {"k": "mkt", "l": "", "t": "Saturday suits Copenhagen. Torvehallerne first, the rest if you have appetite.", "p": [{"name": "Torvehallerne", "rate": "must", "desc": "Two glass halls at Nørreport, sixty-odd stalls, open Saturday. The easiest good lunch in the city and the Coffee Collective bar is inside."}, {"name": "Illums Bolighus", "rate": "good", "desc": "Danish design under one roof on Amagertorv. Hay House above it is the affordable version."}, {"name": "Spice Mart · Vesterbrogade 93", "rate": "good", "desc": "Central and well stocked for Indian groceries — spices, rice, chapati flour, dals, ghee, fresh Indian vegetables."}, {"name": "Reffen street food", "rate": "if", "desc": "Seasonal and likely closed by mid-October — check before crossing the harbour."}]}, {"k": "cafe", "l": "", "t": "Copenhagen is quietly one of the best coffee cities on earth, and Saturday suits it.", "p": [{"name": "Coffee Collective", "rate": "must", "desc": "The shop that started Nordic light roasting in 2008. There is a bar inside Torvehallerne, where you will already be."}, {"name": "Democratic Coffee", "rate": "must", "desc": "Inside the Central Library on Krystalgade. Locally legendary croissants and a queue that moves fast."}, {"name": "Conditori La Glace", "rate": "good", "desc": "Denmark's oldest confectioner, 1870. Layer cakes under glass, staff in white, no concession to modernity."}, {"name": "Andersen & Maillard", "rate": "if", "desc": "A former bank in Nørrebro, now café, roastery and bakery. The cube croissant is the famous one. Expect a queue."}, {"name": "Juno the Bakery", "rate": "if", "desc": "The cardamom bun people cross the city for, out in Østerbro. A genuine detour."}]}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Reffen street food, Refshaleöen", "t": "The main courtyard on a Saturday runs 8–12 open stalls until 22:00 — halloumi burger 95 DKK, fish taco 110 DKK, poke bowl 130 DKK. Bring the toddler; there's a sandpit. Metro M4 to Orientkaj + 15 min walk, or the harbour bus.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Experimentarium & Tivoli", "t": "The **Experimentarium** at Hellerup — three floors of hands-on science and a giant bubble room, the last big outing of the trip. Then **Tivoli** in full Halloween dress, 20,000 pumpkins and witches pitched at the littlest, open through 1 November. The right way to end it.", "p": []}, {"k": "dusk", "l": "Evening · sunset 18:17", "t": "Nyhavn at blue hour, then Tivoli — opened 1843, second-oldest amusement park in the world, and mid-October is its Halloween season with thousands of carved pumpkins, open late. Repack before you go out.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Booking note", "t": "No car for either Copenhagen night, so book on location rather than parking. Anywhere between Nyhavn and Nørreport puts Torvehallerne, Tivoli and the metro within walking distance.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Tickets", "t": "Book Den Blå Planet online for a timed slot — it sells out on Saturdays. The metro from Kastrup station is one stop from the airport, so it doubles as a dry run for tomorrow.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Louisiana Museum opened 1958 · Denmark's Modern-art landmark", "Danish design after 1945 · Klint, Jacobsen, Wegner shape the century"], "shop": ["Fields Outlet Ørestad · 5 min from Copenhagen Towers", "Louisiana Museum + Design Museum Denmark"], "story": ["Klint chair · Jacobsen egg · Wegner Y-chair — the three icons", "Why Denmark makes chairs — a story about winter light"]}}, {"stay": "—", "car": false, "date": "Sun 18 Oct", "drive": "", "rise": "07:49", "set": "18:15", "transport": {"kind": "fly", "text": "SQ351 11:55 → SIN → SQ207 → MEL 18:10 (Mon 19 Oct)"}, "title": "Home", "parts": [{"k": "dawn", "l": "No dawn · flying home", "t": "Metro at 08:30, check-in by 09:15. SQ351 CPH → SIN 11:55, then a short SIN layover before SQ207 to Melbourne — landing 18:10 local on Monday 19 Oct. Pack the camera in the cabin bag; the descent into Melbourne is worth having it out.", "p": []}, {"k": "main", "l": "The day", "t": "Metro to the airport, fifteen minutes. Check-in by 09:15 for SQ351 at 11:55 gets you a calm morning. Two flights: CPH → SIN (about 12 h), a short SIN layover, then SIN → MEL (~7.5 h). Landing 18:10 local Monday 19 Oct.", "p": []}, {"k": "cheap", "l": "Cheapest eats · Kastrup Terminal 3 — Aroma Bakery", "t": "After security, before the SQ351 gate — salmon smørrebrød 89 DKK, breakfast pastry 45 DKK, coffee 35 DKK. Everything else airside is triple. Sit at the window facing the runway; last Danish breakfast of the trip.", "p": []}, {"k": "kid", "l": "Homeward", "t": "A flying day home to Melbourne — the only plan is the airport and the long haul. Let her run off the last of the trip before you board; there is nothing to see now but the road to the gate.", "p": []}, {"k": "tip", "l": "Timing", "t": "Metro to the airport is 15 minutes and runs every few minutes. Check-in by 09:15 for 11:55 gives you a calm morning rather than a scramble.", "p": []}], "spot": {"hist": ["Kastrup opened 1925 · the world's first purpose-built commercial airport", "SQ351 CPH → SIN route · in continuous operation since 1969"], "shop": ["Kastrup duty-free · Aalborg Aquavit, Lakrids liquorice, Anthon Berg", "Last-chance LEGO at the airside stand"], "story": ["Andersen's \"The Nightingale\" · a Danish flying story for the plane", "How Copenhagen invented the modern airport"]}}], "actions": [{"when": "Now", "name": "Cancel three Sixt reservations", "detail": "Harstad/Narvik #9737993875 · Bergen Flesland #9737975717 · Munich #9737974369. All confirmed, all duplicated by Hertz, all carry no-show fees.", "ref": "", "tag": "Act"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "Vignettes, tolls and the cross-border fee", "detail": "Austrian digital vignette (~€12/10 days) and Czech vignette at edalnice.cz, both before you cross. Hertz charges €41.65 when the car leaves Germany, not included in the €848. Norwegian tolls bill automatically to the rental.", "ref": "", "tag": "Act"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "Reserve seats on the Berlin flight", "detail": "XTUPCV, 16 Oct, departs 21:55. Not reserved, and it is bedtime for a three-year-old.", "ref": "", "tag": "Act"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "Winter tyres in writing on the Evenes car", "detail": "Snow reaches the E10 passes by early October some years, and fleets do not always swap until mid-month. Get it confirmed on the booking, not verbally at the desk.", "ref": "", "tag": "Open"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "Book Copenhagen Zoo · 17 Oct", "detail": "Online is cheaper and skips the queue. Arctic Ring before 14:00. Under-4s free.", "ref": "", "tag": "Open"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "eSIMs, offline maps, and a power plan", "detail": "eSIMs bought — install them at home over wifi and confirm Norway is explicitly included, as it is not in the EU and several Europe plans exclude it. Download offline maps for Lofoten, Bergen, Bavaria, the Salzkammergut and Bohemia. Type F plugs throughout except Denmark, which is type K — one universal adaptor covers both.", "ref": "", "tag": "Open"}, {"when": "Before\nyou fly", "name": "Child car seat confirmed on all three rentals", "detail": "Booked on the Norwegian cars. Check the Munich contract — a three-year-old needs a seat in Germany, Austria and Czechia, and turning up without one is a same-day problem.", "ref": "", "tag": "Open"}], "bookings": [{"head": "Flights", "rows": [{"when": "Sun\n27 Sep", "name": "Melbourne → Singapore", "detail": "Singapore Airlines SQ248 · 05:55 → 11:45 · all three passengers · seats 57J/H/K", "ref": "EBOXNZ", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Sun\n27 Sep", "name": "Singapore → Copenhagen", "detail": "Singapore Airlines SQ352 · 00:05 (28 Sep) → 07:10 · seats 56J/H/K", "ref": "EBOXNZ", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Wed\n30 Sep", "name": "Copenhagen → Evenes", "detail": "SAS SK2494 · 08:55 → 11:10 · all three passengers", "ref": "XTG5K5", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Mon\n5 Oct", "name": "Evenes → Oslo", "detail": "Norwegian DY365 · 15:40 → 17:25 · LowFare, seats reserved", "ref": "Y2SH3O", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Sat\n10 Oct", "name": "Bergen → Munich", "detail": "Widerøe WF1868 · 10:50 → 13:15 · SMART fare · NOK 5,196", "ref": "XTLL5F", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Fri\n16 Oct", "name": "Berlin → Copenhagen", "detail": "Norwegian D83305 · 21:55 → 22:55 · LowFare · seats not reserved", "ref": "XTUPCV", "tag": "Seats"}, {"when": "Sun\n18 Oct", "name": "Copenhagen → Singapore", "detail": "Singapore Airlines SQ351 · 11:55 → Singapore, then SQ (connects to SQ207) · redemption booking · all three passengers", "ref": "EBOXNZ", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "Mon\n19 Oct", "name": "Singapore → Melbourne", "detail": "Singapore Airlines SQ207 · 07:50 → 18:10 · seats 54A/B/C · arrive Melbourne late afternoon", "ref": "EBOXNZ", "tag": "Booked"}]}, {"head": "Rail", "rows": [{"when": "Tue\n6 Oct", "name": "Oslo S → Bergen · Bergensbanen", "detail": "Departs 12:03, arrives Bergen about 19:00 · 7h across the Hardangervidda · sit on the left", "ref": "AUB-EMD-HY6", "tag": "Booked"}]}, {"head": "Cars — all Hertz, all with Super Cover", "rows": [{"when": "30 Sep\n→ 5 Oct", "name": "Evenes ⇄ Evenes · Volvo EX40", "detail": "12:00 → 14:00 (amended) · electric · child seat · unlimited km · NOK 5,388 + 581", "ref": "L679E078187", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "8 Oct\n→ 10 Oct", "name": "Bergen Ap & Ssk ⇄ same · Tesla Model Y", "detail": "09:00 → 09:00 · Flyplassvegen 555, the airport depot · NOK 3,204", "ref": "L679E09C104", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "10 Oct\n→ 16 Oct", "name": "Munich Airport → Berlin · Mercedes C-Class", "detail": "14:30 → ~19:00 · snow tyres · €20 drop fee · €848\nPlus €41.65 cross-border fee, not in the quote", "ref": "L679E0E4399", "tag": "Booked"}]}, {"head": "Where you sleep", "rows": [{"when": "28 – 29\nSep", "name": "Hotel Mayfair, Copenhagen · 2 nights", "detail": "The New Standard room · 2 adults · non-refundable · Klook voucher required at check-in. Paid AU$590.80 with 20% Klook cashback (~AU$118 returning as Klook credit) — net cost AU$472.64.", "addr": "Helgolandsgade 3, 1653 Copenhagen, Denmark", "ref": "Klook QBA834491", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "30 Sep\n→ 3 Oct", "name": "Airbnb Elisabeth-bua, Sørvågen · 3 nights", "detail": "Entire home/apt · host Øyvind (+47 95 82 09 10) · self check-in with key safe. Ten minutes further west of Reine on Moskenesøya — quiet spit above the fjord, close to the ferries at Å.", "addr": "Besselvågveien, 8392 Sørvågen, Norway", "ref": "Airbnb HMW9EP35QF · confirmed 8 Sep", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "3 – 4\nOct", "name": "Aurora nights — flexible · 2 nights", "detail": "Svolvær hotel cancelled. Kept open to chase clear skies: Polar Park in Bardu (~5h east from Reine) for the wildlife, and — if Lofoten stays cloudy — push another 90 minutes east over the border to Abisko in Swedish Lapland, the driest aurora sky in Scandinavia. Book the room on the day once the aurora forecast and cloud map are in.", "addr": "Route options: Polar Park (Bardu, NO) · Narvik · Abisko (SE) via E10", "ref": "—", "tag": "Flex"}, {"when": "5 Oct", "name": "Comfort Hotel Børsparken, Oslo · 1 night", "detail": "Central Oslo, 8 min walk to Oslo S. Superior Double Room with a sofa bed, breakfast for three included. Drop the car at the airport before, no car this night.", "addr": "Tollbugata 4, 0152 Oslo, Norway", "ref": "Expedia 73538897598641", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "6 – 8\nOct", "name": "Central Stay Apartment 201-A, Bergen · 2 nights", "detail": "Airbnb, hosts Asif and Diane. No car needed either night", "addr": "Kong Oscars gate 21 area, Bergen, Norway (confirm exact addr in Airbnb app)", "ref": "Airbnb · confirmed 13 Aug", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "8 Oct", "name": "Soltun Tinyhouse i Flåm · 1 night", "detail": "Airbnb, entire tiny-house on a smallholding above Flåm. Self check-in with a key safe after 5:00 pm. Four beds, host Janicke. Quieter than Flåm village, ten minutes down to Stegastein and the fjord.", "addr": "Vollsgjera 11, 5743 Flåm, Norway", "ref": "Airbnb HM52CWJBQN · confirmed 31 Aug", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "9 Oct", "name": "Scandic Flesland Airport, Bergen · 1 night", "detail": "Right at the airport for the early Munich flight. Standard Double Room, breakfast included. Removes eighty minutes from the front of the 10th.", "addr": "Lønningsveien 9, 5258 Blomsterdalen, Bergen, Norway", "ref": "Expedia 73538916995091", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "10 Oct", "name": "Dorint Sporthotel Garmisch-Partenkirchen · 1 night", "detail": "Comfort Studio with a sofa bed, continental breakfast. Just south of Farchant, twenty-five minutes from Geroldsee — still lets you be at the lake before first light. Check-in from 4:30 pm.", "addr": "Mittenwalder Strasse 59, 82467 Garmisch-Partenkirchen, Germany", "ref": "Expedia 73538901053233", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "11 Oct", "name": "COOEE alpin Hotel Dachstein, Gosau · 1 night", "detail": "Gosau, ~30 min from Hallstatt via the western lake road. Free parking, alpine setting, the Gosausee-Dachstein view five minutes from the door", "addr": "Gosauseestraße 15, 4824 Gosau, Austria", "ref": "6878627337 · PIN 6841", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "12 – 15\nOct", "name": "Wenceslas Square apartment, Prague · 3 nights", "detail": "Airbnb, host Annie · free parking, which was the hardest booking of the trip. Leave the car there until the 15th", "addr": "Jungmannova 742/22, 110 00 Nové Město, Praha", "ref": "Airbnb · confirmed 13 Aug", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "15 Oct", "name": "Amedia Plaza Dresden · 1 night", "detail": "Altstadt — Brühl's Terrace and the Frauenkirche on your doorstep. Double Room, city tax (~€8.24) paid at the desk.", "addr": "Juedenhof 9, Altstadt, 01067 Dresden, Germany", "ref": "Expedia 73538911194693", "tag": "Booked"}, {"when": "16 – 17\nOct", "name": "Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers · 2 nights", "detail": "By IHG. Standard Room with a king bed, breakfast for two. Ten minutes on the M2 metro from Kastrup — one stop, straight from the terminal.", "addr": "Ørestads Boulevard 114-118, 2300 Copenhagen, Denmark", "ref": "Expedia 73538908924274", "tag": "Booked"}]}], "grocery": [{"head": "Everyday groceries", "rows": [{"when": "30 Sep", "name": "Leknes — the big shop", "detail": "Coop Extra and Rema 1000, both on the E10 about an hour short of Reine, and the last full-size supermarkets before the road west. Stock three days here. October is peak Norwegian lamb; the salmon and prawns are excellent and cheap by Australian standards.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "3–4 Oct", "name": "Svolvær", "detail": "Coop and Rema in town, plus a Vinmonopolet if you want wine — alcohol above 4.7% is state-monopoly only in Norway, and shops close early.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "6–9 Oct", "name": "Bergen and the fjords", "detail": "Bergen Storsenter by the bus station has a supermarket under cover. Village shops at Flåm, Aurland and Øystese are small and shut by 17:00–18:00 — buy before you leave Bergen on the 8th.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "10–14 Oct", "name": "Austria, Bavaria and Czechia", "detail": "Billa and Spar in Austria, Edeka and Rewe in Germany, Albert and Billa in Czechia. All far cheaper than Norway. Everything closes on Sunday in Germany and Austria — the 11th is a Sunday, so buy on the Saturday or use petrol-station shops.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "Indian groceries — by city", "rows": [{"when": "Bergen\n6–8 Oct", "name": "Global Food · Vestre Strømkaien 1", "detail": "Regulars rate this the best store of its kind in the city centre, and it is directly across from Bergen Storsenter and the bus station — so it folds into your car-free days. Spices, dals, atta, rice, frozen paratha. Eksotisk Torg nearby is the alternative.", "ref": "The one Indian-grocery stop that fits the itinerary naturally", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Oslo\n5 Oct", "name": "Grønland", "detail": "One stop from Oslo S on the T-bane, and the South Asian quarter of the city — a dozen grocers within a few streets. Only worth it if your flight lands early enough; you arrive 17:25 and most close around 20:00.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Prague\n12–15 Oct", "name": "Indian grocers around the centre", "detail": "Prague has several well-stocked shops within tram distance of Wenceslas Square. You have three nights and an apartment, so this is the easiest place on the trip to cook a proper meal.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Copenhagen\n16–17 Oct", "name": "Vesterbro and Nørrebro", "detail": "Spice Mart, Vesterbrogade 93, is central and well stocked. There are many Indo-Pak, South Asian and Afghan grocers across the city carrying spices, rice, chapati flour, dals, ghee, pickles, fresh Indian vegetables and sweets. Dhadra is the one people name for fresh curry leaves, asafoetida and baby aubergine.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}], "food": [{"head": "For us · no beef, no pork", "rows": [{"when": "Rule", "name": "Read past the meat", "detail": "The guide below lists the classic dishes of each country, including the beef and pork ones — kept in so you know what you are being offered and can say no politely. Skip: svíčková, vepřo-knedlo-zelo, frikadeller, stegt flæsk, Wiener Schnitzel (veal), Tafelspitz, Weisswurst, Leberkäse, Schweinshaxe, currywurst, salami/prosciutto boards, most Central-European sausages.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Order", "name": "What works everywhere", "detail": "Fish is the safest anchor across the whole route: skrei, torsk, laks and reker in Norway; sild, røgede fisk and rejer in Denmark; forelle in the Alps; Třeboň carp and pstruh in Czechia; Elbe zander in Saxony. Chicken (kylling / Hähnchen / kuře) is on every menu. Lamb is peak season in October in Norway. Vegetarian defaults: käsespätzle, smažený sýr, bramboráky, kulajda, quarkkeulchen, halloumi and falafel plates in every capital.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "Norway · 30 Sep – 10 Oct", "rows": [{"when": "Savoury", "name": "Fish soup, skrei and lamb", "detail": "Bergensk fiskesuppe — a creamy fish soup with root vegetables, and the thing to order at Mathallen. Fiskekaker and fiskeboller, fish cakes and balls, are everyday food and sold in every supermarket. Skrei is Arctic cod, though the season is January–April, so October gives you regular torsk. Fårikål, mutton and cabbage, is Norway's national dish and October is exactly its month. Kongekrabbe — king crab — is a splurge in Lofoten and worth one.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Sweet", "name": "Skillingsbolle and brunost", "detail": "Skillingsbolle, the Bergen cinnamon bun — larger and less sweet than a Danish one. Brunost, the caramel-brown whey cheese, on waffles with sour cream and jam: Undredal's goat version on the 8th is the best you will find. Krumkake and lefse in bakeries. Multekrem — cloudberries and cream — if you see it.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Avoid", "name": "The pork traps", "detail": "Pinnekjøtt is lamb ribs and fine; ribbe and medisterkaker are pork. Most hotel breakfast buffets lean heavily on cured pork — the fish, eggs and cheese side is always there.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "Bavaria & Austria · 10 – 12 Oct", "rows": [{"when": "Savoury", "name": "Käsespätzle and forelle", "detail": "Käsespätzle — egg noodles baked with mountain cheese and fried onions, vegetarian and enormous. Kaspressknödel, fried cheese dumplings in broth. Forelle, trout, is the alpine fish and usually Müllerin (pan-fried) or blau. Schlutzkrapfen, spinach-and-cheese half-moons. Wiener Schnitzel is veal by law — the cheap ones labelled Schnitzel Wiener Art are pork, so ask.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Sweet", "name": "Kaiserschmarrn — order this", "detail": "Kaiserschmarrn, shredded fluffy pancake with plum compote and icing sugar, is the correct thing to eat in the Salzkammergut with a three-year-old, and it is often a main course in size. Apfelstrudel with vanilla sauce. Salzburger Nockerl, a soufflé shaped like the local peaks. Zaunerstollen at Bad Ischl on the 12th — imperial court confectioner since 1832. Germknödel, a steamed yeast dumpling filled with plum jam under melted butter and poppy seed.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "Czechia · 12 – 15 Oct", "rows": [{"when": "Savoury", "name": "Harder here — but three good ones", "detail": "Czech cooking is built on pork and beef, so aim at these: smažený sýr, a thick slab of fried Edam with tartare sauce, which is a national institution and entirely vegetarian. Kulajda, a creamy dill-and-mushroom soup with a poached egg. Bramboráky, garlicky potato pancakes. Třeboň carp on the 12th if you pass through — the fishponds have been farmed since the 1500s. Svíčková is beef and vepřo-knedlo-zelo is pork; both are the classics you will be offered.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Sweet", "name": "Trdelník, and what to have instead", "detail": "Trdelník is the spiral chimney cake sold everywhere in Prague — it is fun, she will love it, and Czechs will tell you it is not actually Czech. Better: ovocné knedlíky, fruit dumplings with quark and butter, and medovník, a dense honey layer cake. Větrník, a caramel choux, is the pastry locals actually buy.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "Germany & Denmark · 15 – 18 Oct", "rows": [{"when": "Dresden", "name": "Eierschecke and Stollen", "detail": "Dresdner Eierschecke, a three-layer quark and custard cake, is the local sweet and hard to find outside Saxony. Stollen is a Dresden invention — the proper season is Advent but bakeries sell it year-round. Savoury: Quarkkeulchen, sweet potato-quark fritters, work as either.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Copenhagen", "name": "Smørrebrød — pick carefully", "detail": "Smørrebrød is the thing to eat, and the fish versions are the best ones anyway: stjerneskud (fried and steamed plaice with prawns), rejemad (piled cold prawns), and pickled herring in several cures. Avoid flæskesteg and leverpostej, which are pork. Sweet: kanelsnegle, the cinnamon snail, and the cardamom bun at Juno. Wienerbrød — what the world calls a Danish — at any bakery.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}], "light": [["28 Sep", "07:06", "Copenhagen · arrival", "18:55", "Nyhavn · if awake"], ["29 Sep", "07:08", "Nyhavn · empty & gold", "18:52", "Ofelia Plads · harbour"], ["30 Sep", "—", "in transit", "18:50", "Hamnøy bridge · 5 min · scout"], ["1 Oct", "07:29", "Hamnøy bridge · 5 min", "18:46", "Sakrisøy · 5 min"], ["2 Oct", "07:32", "Reinebringen, or Hamnøy", "18:42", "Sakrisøy · aurora at Skagsanden"], ["3 Oct", "07:32", "Hamnøy · last chance", "18:32", "Uttakleiv · on route"], ["4 Oct", "07:34", "Tjeldbergtind, or Svolvær harbour", "18:28", "Svolvær harbour · walkable"], ["5 Oct", "07:37", "Svolvær, then Sildpollnes on the way out", "—", "flying"], ["6 Oct", "07:41", "from the train — Hardangervidda from 15:00", "19:02", "Bryggen blue hour if the train is on time"], ["7 Oct", "08:19", "Bryggen alleys · walkable", "18:59", "Nordnes point · 20 min walk"], ["8 Oct", "08:22", "Bryggen again, before the car", "18:56", "Stegastein · 640 m above the fjord"], ["9 Oct", "08:12", "Stegastein or the Aurlandsfjord", "18:57", "Steinsdalsfossen, then on to the airport"], ["10 Oct", "—", "flying", "18:38", "Neuschwanstein · Marienbrücke, west façade lit"], ["11 Oct", "07:35", "Geroldsee · Karwendel alpenglow · 20 min", "18:36", "Hallstatt · after the coaches leave"], ["12 Oct", "07:38", "Hallstatt viewpoint · lakeside road, 10 min", "18:33", "Krumlov castle terrace, Cloak Bridge"], ["13 Oct", "07:39", "Charles Bridge, empty · 15 min walk", "18:27", "Letná Park · the whole city"], ["14 Oct", "07:41", "Vyšehrad · fortress, ramparts, empty", "18:25", "Charles Bridge or the embankment"], ["15 Oct", "07:43", "Prague · third and last dawn", "18:14", "Prague · dinner, then drive to Dresden"], ["16 Oct", "07:31", "Augustusbrücke · Altstadt across the Elbe", "18:07", "on the road — leave Dresden by 16:30"], ["17 Oct", "07:47", "sleep in — you landed at 23:00", "18:17", "Nyhavn blue hour · then Tivoli"]], "kid": {"title": "For the three-year-old", "intro": "Filtered for a Melbourne family. You already have Melbourne Zoo, Werribee, Healesville, SEA LIFE, Scienceworks and Luna Park's 1912 Scenic Railway — so a merely-good aquarium is a wasted day.", "items": [{"k": "li", "t": "Lofotr Viking Museum · 3 Oct. An 83-metre longhouse with fires lit and costumed interpreters. Nothing comparable exists in Australia."}, {"k": "li", "t": "The Bergensbanen · 6 Oct. Seven hours across a mountain plateau, with a dining car and room to move. Far easier at three than a car."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Fløibanen · 7 Oct. Eight minutes up, troll forest walk and goats at the top."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Undredal goats · 8 Oct. A hundred people, more goats, and the cheese they make from them."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Steinsdalsfossen · 9 Oct. You walk behind the waterfall on a made path. The best twenty minutes of the Norwegian leg for her."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Neuschwanstein · 10 Oct. The castle that Disney copied, and she is exactly the right age for that to mean something."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Hallstatt funicular and Skywalk · 11 Oct. Open to all ages, unlike the salt mine below it."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Petřín Hill, Prague · 13 Oct. Funicular, mirror maze, lookout tower, gardens down. The best afternoon in Prague at her age."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Prague Zoo or Kutná Hora · 14 Oct. The zoo is world-class, on a hillside with a chairlift, reachable by boat up the Vltava."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Polar bears at Copenhagen Zoo · 29 Sep. The Arctic Ring puts an acrylic tunnel through their pool, so they swim overhead. Melbourne Zoo has no polar bears and never will — the big day-two outing, taken while everyone is fresh."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Experimentarium · 17 Oct. Copenhagen's hands-on science museum — three floors and a giant bubble room, the last outing before the flight home. Then Tivoli in Halloween season to finish."}, {"k": "li", "t": "Skip: Bergen Aquarium, VilVite. Fine, but none better than what is twenty minutes from your house."}]}, "wear": {"title": "What to wear", "intro": "This trip has two different cold problems, and most packing lists only solve one. Hiking Kvalvika you generate heat and need to shed it. Standing at a tripod at Hamnøy at 07:15, or waiting for the aurora at Skagsanden at 22:00, you generate nothing — and that is where people get genuinely cold. Pack for the standing still, not the walking.", "items": [{"k": "h", "t": "What you are actually dressing for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lofoten, 30 Sep – 5 Oct. Highs of 8 °C, nights down to 2 °C, and it is the wettest month of the year — rain on roughly 27 days of the month, about 160 mm, and when it rains it comes down hard rather than drizzling. Average wind runs near 30 km/h off the Atlantic. Snow is possible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bergen and the fjords, 6–10 Oct. Milder, around 10 °C, but Bergen averages over 200 wet days a year and you have two walking days there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Austria and Bohemia, 10–16 Oct. Cold mornings near freezing in the Salzkammergut, mild afternoons around 12–15 °C, much drier. Prague and Dresden are city walking in autumn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number that matters: 3 °C with 30 km/h of wind and wet clothing feels like about −5 °C. That is your aurora night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Five things people forget"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spare gloves and socks in the car, always. The single most useful thing in this list. Wet hands end a shoot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dry bag or two. For camera gear at Steinsdalsfossen and in the Lammerklamm, where you walk through spray, and for wet clothes going back in the boot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Swimwear. Some rorbuer have saunas, and it would be a shame to find one and not be able to use it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something reasonable for Prague and Vienna dinners. Not formal, but you will not want to eat at Café Savoy in wet hiking trousers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A packable duffel. Eighteen nights, five countries, and you will buy things — Undredal cheese, Hardanger cider, Gmundner ceramics. Something that folds flat and comes back full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three layers, and what they must be"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extremities, which is where it actually goes wrong"}, {"k": "h", "t": "For the three-year-old"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The honest summary. If you buy only three things for this trip, make them a proper taped hardshell each, a Reima or Didriksons waterproof overall for her, and enough merino socks that nobody ever has to put on a wet pair. Everything else you can improvise; those three you cannot."}]}, "verify": {"title": "Verify before you go", "intro": "", "items": [{"k": "p", "t": "Aurlandsfjellet Snow Road and Stalheimskleiva — both summer roads that close with early snow. The Voss fallback adds about 40 minutes. Check vegvesen.no on the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ulvik cider farm hours in October. Small family operations, and they contract sharply outside summer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Norwegian cars are electric — Volvo EX40 and Tesla Model Y. Charge overnight at the accommodation; Leknes and Svolvær both have fast chargers on the 5 Oct run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Marienbrücke access on 10 October — free, but it closes without notice for weather or maintenance and mid-October is the edge of its season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Krippenstein cable car season end date, if you want Five Fingers from Obertraun."}]}, "wearGroups": [{"head": "", "rows": [{"when": "Base", "name": "Merino wool — never cotton", "detail": "Cotton holds water against the skin and stops insulating the moment you sweat, which is why it is genuinely dangerous in wind. Merino keeps working wet and does not smell after four days, which matters when you are living out of one bag for eighteen nights.\n\n\n      Weight: 200 gsm for daytime, 260 gsm for the aurora nights and dawn shoots.\n\n      Examples: Icebreaker 200 Oasis or 260 Tech (widely stocked in Australia), Kathmandu or Macpac merino as the cheaper equivalent, Smartwool Classic Thermal.\n\n      Pack: two tops and two bottoms each. Wash one, wear one.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Mid", "name": "Fleece for moving, down for standing still", "detail": "You want both, and they do different jobs. A grid fleece breathes while you walk uphill; a light down or synthetic puffer is what you pull on when you stop and the wind finds you.\n\n\n      Fleece: Patagonia R1, Arc'teryx Delta, Macpac Nitro. Grid-back construction, not a flat fleece.\n\n      Puffer: Patagonia Down Sweater, Rab Microlight Alpine, Uniqlo Ultra Light Down as the budget option. Synthetic fill (Primaloft) if you can — it keeps insulating damp, and Lofoten will be damp.\n\n      Pack: one of each per adult.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Shell", "name": "Hardshell, taped seams, hood that fits over a hat", "detail": "This is the one thing not to economise on. A water-resistant softshell will fail in a Lofoten squall — you want a genuine waterproof-breathable membrane, fully taped, with pit zips.\n\n\n      Examples: Norrøna Falketind or Trollveggen (Norwegian, made for exactly this), Arc'teryx Beta AR, Rab Kinetic, Kathmandu Bealey as the value option. Gore-Tex or equivalent 3-layer.\n\n      Trousers matter too: waterproof overtrousers you can pull on over jeans. Rab Downpour, Montbell Versalite. Light enough to live in the camera bag.\n\n      Pack: one jacket and one pair of overtrousers each, no exceptions.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "", "rows": [{"when": "Feet", "name": "Waterproof boots, ankle support, merino socks", "detail": "Kvalvika is wet rock and a saddle crossing; Reinebringen is 1,566 sherpa steps. Trail runners will not do.\n\n\n      Boots: Salomon Quest 4 GTX, Scarpa Terra GTX, Meindl Bhutan. Break them in before you fly.\n\n      Socks: Darn Tough or Icebreaker hiking weight, merino, four pairs each. Wet feet ruin a day faster than anything else.\n\n      Also: one pair of comfortable town shoes for Prague, Dresden and Copenhagen — you will walk a lot on cobbles.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Hands", "name": "Two pairs, and one must work a camera", "detail": "Thick gloves are useless at a tripod and thin gloves are useless at −5 wind chill. The answer is a liner plus an overmitt you can pull off.\n\n\n      Liner: merino or touchscreen-compatible photography gloves — Vallerret Markhof Pro, or Icebreaker liners.\n\n      Over: insulated mitts, warmer than gloves because the fingers share heat. Hestra Fall Line, Black Diamond Mercury.\n\n      Add: chemical hand warmers for the aurora nights. They also keep camera batteries alive.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Head\n& neck", "name": "Beanie and a Buff, per person", "detail": "Merino beanie that covers the ears, and a neck gaiter — a Buff Merino Lightweight does more than its size suggests and doubles as a hat, a headband and a face cover in wind.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}, {"head": "", "rows": [{"when": "Base", "name": "Merino, two sets", "detail": "Polarn O. Pyret and Icebreaker both do 200 gsm merino from toddler sizes. Worth the money because it keeps working when she inevitably gets wet, and it does not itch the way lambswool does.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Mid", "name": "Fleece overall or a two-piece set", "detail": "Reima or Didriksons — Finnish and Swedish, built for children who play outdoors in this exact weather, and stocked in Australia. A one-piece fleece is easier than separates when you are dressing her in a car park.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Shell", "name": "A proper waterproof overall — the single best purchase", "detail": "Reima Tec or Didriksons Slaskeman. A full-body waterproof with taped seams means she can sit on wet rock at Haukland, get rained on at Steinsdalsfossen, and walk behind a waterfall without any of it mattering. Scandinavian children live in these. Buy one size up and roll the cuffs.\n\n\n      Add: waterproof mittens on a cord through the sleeves — she will lose them otherwise — and a fleece-lined hat that ties under the chin so it survives the wind.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Feet", "name": "Insulated wellies plus warm boots", "detail": "Two pairs: rubber boots with a wool liner for wet days and beaches, and a warm waterproof boot for walking. Bogs, Reima or Kuoma. Four pairs of merino socks, same reasoning as yours.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}, {"when": "Carrier", "name": "A rain cover and a footmuff", "detail": "Most framed carriers take an integrated rain and sun hood — get the one for yours. A footmuff or even a small down blanket tucked round her legs makes the difference between an hour outside and twenty minutes.", "ref": "", "tag": ""}]}], "budget": {"currency": "AUD", "note": "Every line here comes from an actual receipt in your inbox. No estimates. NOK 1 = AU$0.139; EUR 1 = AU$1.654. Airbnb credit is counted as AUD spend — it was bought with real money. Cashback rates (Expedia 16%, Airbnb 5%, Freely 20%) are tracked per row and totalled at the top.", "categories": [{"head": "Air travel", "note": "Long-haul on SQ was a KrisFlyer redemption — 378,000 miles for three, AU$854 cash for taxes. Four intra-Europe legs in cash.", "rows": [{"when": "27 Sep – 19 Oct", "name": "MEL ⇄ CPH · SQ248/352 + SQ351/207 · redemption", "detail": "378,000 KrisFlyer miles + AU$854 taxes on Amex. Three passengers, round-trip.", "cash": 854, "points": 378000, "ref": "EBOXNZ", "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "30 Sep", "name": "Copenhagen → Evenes · SAS SK2494", "detail": "EUR 651.25 total (fare 448 + extras 21 + tax 182.25) · 3 pax · Mastercard ...8524", "cash": 1077, "ref": "XTG5K5", "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "5 Oct", "name": "Evenes → Oslo · Norwegian DY365", "detail": "EUR 332.64 (2×206.92 adult + 93.48 child + 7.40 fee) · Mastercard ...8524", "cash": 550, "ref": "Y2SH3O", "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "10 Oct", "name": "Bergen → Munich · Widerøe WF1868", "detail": "NOK 5,196 (2×1,839 adult + 1,518 child) · Mastercard ...8524", "cash": 722, "ref": "XTLL5F", "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "16 Oct", "name": "Berlin → Copenhagen · Norwegian D83305", "detail": "EUR 266.54 (2×92.08 adult + 77.08 child + 5.30 fee) · Mastercard ...8524", "cash": 441, "ref": "XTUPCV", "tag": "Paid"}]}, {"head": "Rail", "rows": [{"when": "6 Oct", "name": "Oslo → Bergen · Vy Bergensbanen · Flex Refundable", "detail": "NOK 2,476 (2×1,238 adult + 0 child 0–5) · Mastercard", "cash": 344, "ref": "AUB-EMD-HY6", "tag": "Paid"}]}, {"head": "Airport transfers", "note": "Melbourne home ⇄ MEL. Both are fixed-fare with AmmuTravels — no prepayment, paid on card after each ride.", "rows": [{"when": "27 Sep · 02:30", "name": "Home → MEL Airport · SQ248", "detail": "$88 fixed fare (GST incl.) · 8-seat Kia Carnival · baby seat · paid after trip on card", "cash": 88, "ref": "AMMU-260907-TX35", "tag": "Scheduled"}]}, {"head": "Cars", "note": "Three Hertz rentals, all charged to Amex ...1003. Super Cover declined on all three — the collision excess is already covered by the Freely policy. Cross-border €41.65 is documented in the Munich contract, charged at pickup.", "rows": [{"when": "30 Sep – 5 Oct", "name": "Evenes ⇄ Evenes · Volvo EX40 (EV)", "detail": "NOK 4,497.57 (was 5,387.57 — Super Cover 890 declined; rental-excess covered by Freely) · 5 days · child seat · unlimited km", "cash": 625, "ref": "L679E078187", "tag": "Paid", "pendingSaving": 124}, {"when": "8 – 10 Oct", "name": "Bergen ⇄ Bergen · Tesla Model Y", "detail": "NOK 2,847.77 (was 3,203.77 — Super Cover 356 declined; rental-excess covered by Freely) · 2 days · child seat · Flesland depot", "cash": 396, "ref": "L679E09C104", "tag": "Paid", "pendingSaving": 49}, {"when": "10 – 16 Oct", "name": "Munich → Berlin · Mercedes C-Class", "detail": "€630.25 (was 848.47 — Super Cover €218.22 declined; rental-excess covered by Freely) · 1 week · child seat, snow tyres, €20 drop fee", "cash": 1042, "ref": "L679E0E4399", "tag": "Paid", "pendingSaving": 361}, {"when": "10 Oct pickup", "name": "Hertz cross-border fee (DE → AT/CZ)", "detail": "€41.65 · documented in the Munich contract, charged at counter", "cash": 69, "tag": "At desk"}]}, {"head": "Accommodation", "note": "Eleven stays, 20 nights. Airbnb credit counted at full AUD face value.", "rows": [{"when": "28 – 29 Sep", "name": "Hotel Mayfair, Copenhagen · 2 nights", "detail": "The New Standard room · 2 adults · non-refundable. Charged AU$590.80, 20% Klook cashback (~AU$118) coming back as Klook credit → net AU$472.64.", "cash": 473, "ref": "Klook QBA834491", "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "30 Sep – 3 Oct", "name": "Airbnb Elisabeth-bua, Sørvågen · 3 nights · host Øyvind", "detail": "AU$675.12 paid 100% with Airbnb credit ($43.40 + $631.72) · replaces the earlier Det grønne huset booking which was cancelled 8 Sep", "cash": 675, "ref": "Airbnb HMW9EP35QF", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.05}, {"when": "3 – 4 Oct", "name": "Fast Hotel Svolvær · 2 nights", "detail": "Basic Triple Room · self-check-in · Amex", "cash": 409, "ref": "Expedia 73538939386662", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}, {"when": "5 Oct", "name": "Comfort Hotel Børsparken, Oslo · 1 night", "detail": "Superior Double · breakfast · Amex", "cash": 266, "ref": "Expedia 73538897598641", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}, {"when": "6 – 8 Oct", "name": "Airbnb Central Stay Apt 201-A, Bergen · 2 nights · host Asif", "detail": "AU$275.61 scheduled to PayPal on 27 Sep", "cash": 276, "ref": "Airbnb HMCYZR8K9F", "tag": "Scheduled", "cashback": 0.05}, {"when": "8 Oct", "name": "Airbnb Soltun Tinyhouse, Flåm · 1 night · host Janicke", "detail": "AU$353.23 paid 100% with Airbnb credit", "cash": 353, "ref": "Airbnb HM52CWJBQN", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.05}, {"when": "9 Oct", "name": "Scandic Flesland Airport, Bergen · 1 night", "detail": "Standard Double · breakfast · Amex", "cash": 248, "ref": "Expedia 73538916995091", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}, {"when": "10 Oct", "name": "Dorint Sporthotel Garmisch-Partenkirchen · 1 night", "detail": "Comfort Studio · breakfast · €5.65 city tax at desk · Amex", "cash": 289, "ref": "Expedia 73538901053233", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}, {"when": "11 Oct", "name": "COOEE alpin Dachstein, Gosau · 1 night", "detail": "€132.20 (incl. VAT + €8 city tax) · 50% prepay 7 days out, rest at hotel", "cash": 219, "ref": "Booking.com 6878627337", "tag": "Guaranteed"}, {"when": "12 – 15 Oct", "name": "Airbnb Wenceslas Square apt, Prague · 3 nights · host Annie", "detail": "AU$469.94 paid 100% with Airbnb credit", "cash": 470, "ref": "Airbnb HMRHSCNFWN", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.05}, {"when": "15 Oct", "name": "Amedia Plaza Dresden · 1 night", "detail": "Double Room · €8.24 city tax at desk · Amex", "cash": 226, "ref": "Expedia 73538911194693", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}, {"when": "16 – 17 Oct", "name": "Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers · 2 nights", "detail": "Standard King · breakfast for 2 · Amex", "cash": 518, "ref": "Expedia 73538908924274", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.16}]}, {"head": "Pre-trip essentials", "note": "One-off fees paid before departure.", "rows": [{"when": "before 27 Sep", "name": "International Driving Permit · Australia Post / RACV", "detail": "One-year permit — required alongside your Australian licence for Norway, Germany, Austria and Czechia.", "cash": 67, "tag": "Paid"}, {"when": "before 27 Sep", "name": "Travel eSIMs · 3 pax · Europe roaming", "detail": "AU$152 for three eSIMs covering Denmark, Norway, Germany, Austria and Czechia.", "cash": 152, "tag": "Paid"}]}, {"head": "Insurance", "rows": [{"when": "7 Sep", "name": "Freely Travel Insurance · Overseas Explorer Pro", "detail": "AU$562.29 (premium 556.73 + stamp duty 5.56) · 3 pax · 27 Sep – 19 Oct · one existing-condition approved · Amex", "cash": 562, "ref": "Policy 726000271287", "tag": "Paid", "cashback": 0.2}]}]}}